QB.add(46,[{"id":46001,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A 45-year-old smoker feels completely well, but a scan shows early tissue changes caused by disease. Which stage of the natural history of disease has this person reached?","o":["Subclinical","Susceptible","Clinical","Underlying","Recovery"],"e":"The susceptible stage begins with the onset of risk factors, when a person is at risk but has no disease. The subclinical stage begins with the onset of pathological changes, when disease has begun inside the body without symptoms. This person has tissue changes and no symptoms, so they are subclinical. The clinical stage would begin only with symptoms.","w":["Susceptible people have risk factors, but no disease has started.","The clinical stage needs symptoms, and this person feels well.","The underlying stage comes before any risk factors exist.","Recovery is an outcome that follows the clinical stage."],"k":"Subclinical = pathological changes without symptoms.","rn":[["","Secondary prevention targets who?","Secondary prevention targets who?","Individuals with subclinical disease"],["","What are the 5 phases of the natural history of disease?","What are the 5 phases of the natural history of disease?","Underlying; Susceptible; Subclinical; Clinical; Recovery/disability/death"]]},{"id":46002,"t":"Natural History of Disease","s":"Which pairing of a natural history stage with the event that marks it is correct?","o":["Clinical stage: onset of symptoms","Susceptible stage: onset of symptoms","Subclinical stage: onset of risk factors","Clinical stage: onset of pathological changes in the tissue","Underlying stage: onset of pathological changes"],"e":"The susceptible stage is marked by the onset of risk factors. The subclinical stage is marked by the onset of pathological changes. The clinical stage is marked by the onset of symptoms, and the final stage is the outcome of recovery, disability or death. The order is therefore risk factors, silent changes, symptoms, outcome.","w":["Symptoms mark the clinical stage, while risk factors mark the susceptible stage.","Risk factors mark the susceptible stage, and pathological changes mark the subclinical stage.","Pathological changes mark the subclinical stage, which comes before symptoms.","The underlying stage comes before any risk factors or pathological changes."],"k":"Risk factors → pathological changes → symptoms → outcome."},{"id":46003,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"Name the stage of the natural history of disease in which conditions exist that could eventually lead to disease, before any risk factors have appeared.","a":["underlying","underlying stage"],"e":"The underlying stage is when conditions exist that could eventually lead to disease, before any risk factors. It is the target of primordial prevention, which works through social and environmental measures. The susceptible stage follows with the onset of risk factors.","k":"Underlying stage comes before risk factors and is the target of primordial prevention.","rn":[["","Primordial prevention targets which stage of natural disease?","Primordial prevention targets which stage of natural disease?","Underlying stage"]]},{"id":46004,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A government bans smoking indoors and funds new pedestrian trails across a city. Which level of prevention do these measures represent?","o":["Primordial prevention","Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention","Quaternary prevention"],"e":"Primordial prevention targets the population rather than individuals, using social and environmental interventions through legislation or policy changes. It acts at the underlying stage to prevent risk factors developing. Banning indoor smoking and building bike or pedestrian trails are both examples.","w":["Primary prevention targets susceptible people, with measures such as sunscreens and vaccination.","Secondary prevention targets people with subclinical disease through early detection.","Tertiary prevention targets people who already have the disease.","Quaternary prevention protects patients from over-medicalisation."],"k":"Primordial: population-level law and policy, before risk factors exist.","rn":[["","What are 4 general examples of primordial prevention?","What are 4 general examples of primordial prevention?","Better quality housing; Bike or pedestrian trails; Indoor smoking ban; State tobacco control"],["","Primordial prevention targets the population or individuals?","Primordial prevention targets the population or individuals?","The population"],["","Primordial prevention focuses on what type of interventions?","Primordial prevention focuses on what type of interventions?","Social and environmental"]]},{"id":46005,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"Why can a small fall in the prevalence of a risk factor across a whole population be so valuable?","o":["It translates into a large reduction in disease incidence","It removes the need for any later treatment of the disease","It guarantees that every individual at high risk avoids the disease","It raises the sensitivity of every screening test used in that population","It makes the disease subclinical in everyone who would have developed it"],"e":"Primordial prevention focuses on preventing the development of risk factors, upstream of disease detection. Even small reductions in the prevalence of risk factors in the general population translate into large reductions in disease incidence. This is why population-level legislation and policy can have such a broad effect.","w":["Treatment is still needed for people who go on to develop disease.","A population-level change does not guarantee an outcome for each individual.","Sensitivity is a property of the test and is not raised by this change.","The aim is to prevent risk factors, not to convert disease into a silent form."],"k":"Small falls in risk factor prevalence → large falls in incidence.","rn":[["","Why do small reductions in risk factors matter in primordial prevention?","Why do small reductions in risk factors matter in primordial prevention?","Large reductions in disease incidence"]]},{"id":46006,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A healthy teenager receives a vaccination. Which level of prevention is this?","a":["primary","primary prevention"],"e":"Primary prevention targets a susceptible population or individual and aims to prevent a disease from emerging, generally in healthy people. Vaccination reduces exposure to a risk, like sunscreen. Immunising against communicable diseases is one of the examples given.","k":"Vaccination of healthy people = primary prevention.","rn":[["","Primary prevention targets who?","Primary prevention targets who?","Susceptible population or individual; Generally healthy individuals"],["","Sunscreens and vaccinations are examples of which primary prevention approach?","Sunscreens and vaccinations are examples of which primary prevention approach?","Reduced exposure to risk factors"]]},{"id":46007,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A person with diabetes who has no foot symptoms attends for a routine foot check. At which level of prevention does this check mainly sit?","o":["Secondary, because it aims at early detection in a person with risk","Primordial, because it acts through a law that applies to the whole population","Tertiary, because rehabilitation after a stroke uses the same type of check","Quaternary, because it protects the patient from any medical intervention","Primary, because it stops diabetes from emerging in a healthy individual"],"e":"Secondary prevention targets individuals with subclinical disease and emphasises early detection. It includes screening programmes and risk factor management. A foot check for people with diabetes is listed as an example of risk factor management at this level.","w":["A foot check is an individual assessment and not a population law.","Tertiary prevention reduces severity once disease is clinical, as in rehabilitation.","Quaternary prevention aims to prevent harmful interventions in illness without disease.","The person already has diabetes, so this check is not preventing diabetes emerging."],"k":"Secondary prevention: early detection and risk factor management.","rn":[["","What is the emphasis of secondary prevention?","What is the emphasis of secondary prevention?","Early detection"],["","What are 3 risk factor management examples of secondary prevention?","What are 3 risk factor management examples of secondary prevention?","Smoking cessation; Vaccinations in asplenic individuals; Foot check for diabetics"]]},{"id":46008,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A patient attends rehabilitation after a myocardial infarction. What is the main goal of this level of prevention?","o":["To reduce severity and complications of established disease","To detect silent disease before any symptoms appear","To stop risk factors from developing in the wider population","To shield the patient from unnecessary medical investigation","To prevent the disease from emerging in a susceptible person"],"e":"Tertiary prevention targets the clinical and outcome stages of disease. Its goal is to reduce disease severity and associated complications or sequelae. Rehabilitation after myocardial infarction or stroke and chronic disease management are the examples given.","w":["Detecting silent disease is secondary prevention.","Stopping risk factors from developing in a population is primordial prevention.","Protecting a patient from over-medicalisation is quaternary prevention.","Preventing emergence in a susceptible person is primary prevention."],"k":"Tertiary prevention reduces severity and sequelae of established disease.","rn":[["","What does tertiary prevention reduce?","What does tertiary prevention reduce?","Disease severity; Associated complications/sequelae"]]},{"id":46009,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A patient has troubling symptoms but no identifiable disease, and has been offered yet another invasive test with little prospect of benefit. A doctor decides to protect them from this. Which level of prevention is being practised?","o":["Quaternary","Primordial","Secondary","Tertiary","Primary"],"e":"Quaternary prevention targets patients with illness but no disease. It aims to identify patients at risk of over-medicalisation, protect them from new medical invasion and suggest interventions that are ethically acceptable. Too many tests and treatments can themselves cause harm.","w":["Primordial prevention acts on populations through law and policy.","Secondary prevention looks for subclinical disease rather than limiting testing.","Tertiary prevention reduces complications of established disease.","Primary prevention stops a disease emerging in a susceptible person."],"k":"Quaternary: illness without disease, avoid over-medicalisation.","rn":[["","Quaternary prevention targets which patients?","Quaternary prevention targets which patients?","Illness but no disease"],["","What 2 things does quaternary prevention do for a patient at risk?","What 2 things does quaternary prevention do for a patient at risk?","Protect from new medical invasion; Suggest ethically acceptable interventions"],["","Quaternary prevention identifies patients at risk of what?","Quaternary prevention identifies patients at risk of what?","Over-medicalisation"]]},{"id":46010,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"What is the term for too many tests and treatments, which quaternary prevention aims to avoid?","a":["over-medicalisation","overmedicalisation","over medicalisation","over-medicalization","overmedicalization"],"e":"Over-medicalisation means too many tests and treatments, with possible harm. Quaternary prevention identifies patients at risk of it and protects them from new medical invasion. It suggests interventions that are ethically acceptable instead.","k":"Over-medicalisation = too many tests and treatments.","rn":[["","Quaternary prevention identifies patients at risk of what?","Quaternary prevention identifies patients at risk of what?","Over-medicalisation"]]},{"id":46011,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"Which option correctly shows how anti-smoking work can fall into three levels of prevention?","o":["Age limits on sales are primary, lung screening for high-risk people is secondary, reducing impact after disease is tertiary","Age limits on sales are tertiary, lung screening for high-risk people is primary, reducing impact after disease is secondary in every case","Age limits on sales are secondary, lung screening for high-risk people is tertiary, reducing impact after disease is primary","All three are primordial, because they all involve tobacco and each is applied to the whole of the population","All three are secondary, because each needs subclinical disease to be present before it can be offered at all"],"e":"Classification systems are imperfect, and interventions may fall into more than one category. Preventing people starting smoking, for example with age limits on sale, is primary. Offering screening to those at high risk of lung cancer is secondary. Reducing the impact once lung cancer or smoking-related cardiovascular disease has developed is tertiary.","w":["Age limits act before disease, and screening looks for silent disease.","Age limits prevent starting, and reducing impact after disease is tertiary.","These are not all population legislation, and screening is offered to high-risk people.","Age limits act before any disease has developed."],"k":"One intervention area can span primary, secondary and tertiary prevention.","rn":[["","Smoking as secondary prevention?","Smoking as secondary prevention?","Lung cancer screening for high-risk people"],["","Smoking as tertiary prevention?","Smoking as tertiary prevention?","Reducing the impacts; After lung cancer or cardiovascular disease"],["","Smoking as primary prevention?","Smoking as primary prevention?","Do not start smoking; Age limit on cigarette sales"]]},{"id":46012,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A student says that all screening is secondary prevention. Which statement is the best correction?","o":["Screening done before detectable tissue changes works at the level of primary prevention","Screening is always tertiary prevention, because it takes place after symptoms have begun","Screening is always primordial prevention, because it is applied by law to the population","Screening is never prevention, because it is only used to establish a definite diagnosis","Screening is always quaternary prevention, because it removes the need for any further tests"],"e":"Screening programmes sit mainly in secondary prevention, which targets subclinical disease. However, when screening is done before there are detectable tissue changes, it works at the level of primary prevention. The categories overlap, so a single intervention can fall into more than one.","w":["Screening acts before symptom onset, in apparently well people.","Primordial measures act through social and environmental policy before risk factors develop.","A screening test is not intended to be diagnostic, but it is still a form of prevention.","Positive screening results need further diagnostic testing."],"k":"Not all screening is secondary: before tissue changes, it is primary.","rn":[["","Is all screening secondary prevention?","Is all screening secondary prevention?","No; Can be primary if before tissue changes"]]},{"id":46013,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A programme encourages healthy lifestyles in a low-risk population of children to stop risk factors appearing. A separate clinic treats adults already at high risk of cardiovascular disease to stop disease occurring. How should these be classified?","o":["The first is primordial; the second is primary","The first is primary; the second is primordial","Both are primordial, as they involve lifestyle","The first is secondary; the second is tertiary","The first is tertiary; the second is quaternary"],"e":"Primordial prevention focuses on a low-risk population and encourages healthy lifestyles to prevent the development of risk factors. Primary prevention focuses on preventing disease from occurring and usually targets individuals already at high risk of cardiovascular disease. Risk accumulates across life, beginning with fetal and infant health.","w":["This reverses the two levels.","The clinic targets high-risk individuals, which is primary prevention.","Neither programme is aimed at people with existing subclinical or clinical disease.","Neither programme treats established disease or over-medicalisation."],"k":"Primordial: low-risk, prevent risk factors. Primary: high-risk, prevent disease.","rn":[["","What does primordial prevention focus on, in terms of population and lifestyle?","What does primordial prevention focus on, in terms of population and lifestyle?","A low risk population; Encouraging healthy lifestyles; To prevent risk factors developing"],["","What does primary prevention focus on, in terms of disease and risk?","What does primary prevention focus on, in terms of disease and risk?","Preventing disease from occurring; Usually targets people already at high risk of CVD (cardiovascular disease)"]]},{"id":46014,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"Which exposure belongs to the fetal and infant stage of accumulated lifetime cardiovascular risk?","o":["Maternal smoking and maternal gestational diabetes","Occupational exposures and psychosocial stress in adult employment","Rehabilitation after a myocardial infarction later on in adult life","Personal drug use during the years of adult working life","Personal alcohol use during later adolescence"],"e":"Cardiovascular disease starts in early life, with fetal and infant health. Maternal adverse exposures include unhealthy diet, physical inactivity, smoking, non-ideal body weight, hypertension, and pregestational and gestational diabetes mellitus. Individual risks vary according to life experiences and exposure history.","w":["Occupational exposures and psychosocial stress are listed for adulthood.","Rehabilitation is a tertiary intervention and not a fetal exposure.","Personal drug use in adulthood is not a fetal or infant exposure.","Personal alcohol use belongs to the adolescent stage."],"k":"Cardiovascular risk begins with maternal exposures in fetal life.","rn":[["","What maternal adverse exposures harm fetal and infant health? (part 2)","What maternal adverse exposures harm fetal and infant health? (part 2)","Non-ideal body weight; Hypertension; Pregestational and gestational diabetes mellitus"]]},{"id":46015,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"A screening test comes back positive in a well person. Which statement best describes what that result means?","o":["They probably have the disease and should be referred for a gold standard test","They definitely have the disease and should begin treatment straight away without further testing","They definitely do not have the disease, as they do not feel unwell at all","They should be discharged, as a screening test does not produce useful results","They should repeat the same screening test each day until it turns negative"],"e":"Screening is the presumptive identification of unrecognised disease or defect by tests that can be applied rapidly. It sorts apparently well persons who probably have a disease from those who probably do not, and it is not intended to be diagnostic. People with positive or suspicious findings must be referred for diagnosis and necessary treatment by the gold standard test.","w":["A screening test does not establish a definite diagnosis.","Feeling well does not exclude disease, since screening looks for unrecognised disease.","A positive finding is useful and needs referral.","The next step is referral for diagnosis, not repeating until negative."],"k":"Positive screen = probable disease → refer for gold standard diagnosis.","rn":[["","Screening tests sort apparently well persons into which 2 groups?","Screening tests sort apparently well persons into which 2 groups?","Probably have a disease; Probably do not"],["","A positive screening test leads on to what?","A positive screening test leads on to what?","Gold standard test; E.g. biopsy or imaging"]]},{"id":46016,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"What term is used for the best test for confirming a disease, such as a biopsy or particular imaging?","a":["gold standard","gold standard test","the gold standard"],"e":"The gold standard is the best test for confirming a disease, such as a biopsy or particular imaging. A positive screening result goes on to this test. In a 2 x 2 table it provides the truth against which the screening test is compared.","k":"Gold standard confirms disease and defines the truth in a 2 x 2 table.","rn":[["","A positive screening test leads on to what?","A positive screening test leads on to what?","Gold standard test; E.g. biopsy or imaging"],["","In a 2 x 2 table, what forms the true class?","In a 2 x 2 table, what forms the true class?","The gold standard"]]},{"id":46017,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"What does the word \"presumptive\" in the World Health Organisation definition of screening imply?","o":["The result is a best guess that has not yet been proven","The result is a final diagnosis confirmed by a biopsy","The result applies only to patients who have symptoms","The result is presumed negative unless a person is unwell","The result is decided by a physician without doing any test"],"e":"Screening is the presumptive identification of unrecognised disease or defect. Presumptive means a best guess that is not yet proven. Screening provides a probability of potentially having a disease, and it is a rough sorting process that is not 100% accurate.","w":["A biopsy is a gold standard test, which comes after a positive screen.","Screening is applied to apparently well people.","The word does not mean the result is assumed to be negative.","Screening applies tests, examinations or other procedures."],"k":"Presumptive = a best guess; screening gives a probability."},{"id":46018,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Which statement best captures the purpose of screening?","o":["To identify apparently healthy people at higher risk, so early treatment may improve outcomes for some","To confirm disease in symptomatic people, so that treatment can begin on a definite diagnosis","To guarantee a better outcome for everyone who is screened, whatever the result of the test","To replace the need for taking a history and examining patients on later clinic visits","To treat all members of a population before any disease has been suspected or found"],"e":"The purpose of screening is to identify people in an apparently healthy population who are at higher risk of a health problem or condition, so that early treatment or intervention can be offered. This, in turn, may lead to better health outcomes for some of the screened individuals. The benefits do not always outweigh the downsides.","w":["Confirming disease in symptomatic people is the job of diagnostic tests.","Better outcomes are possible for some, and they are not guaranteed for everyone.","Tests are not taken alone, so a history and examination remain necessary.","Screening identifies people for further assessment rather than treating everyone."],"k":"Screening: find higher-risk well people so early intervention may help some.","rn":[["","What is the purpose of screening?","What is the purpose of screening?","Identify higher-risk people in an apparently healthy population; Offer early treatment or intervention"]]},{"id":46019,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"A committee is considering a new screening programme for a condition. There is a good test, but no accepted treatment exists. Which conclusion follows from the Wilson-Jungner criteria?","o":["A criterion is not met, because there should be an accepted treatment for recognised disease","All criteria are met, because a suitable test alone is enough to justify a screening programme","A criterion is not met, because the condition must be rare to justify screening","All criteria are met, because treatment is only considered once screening has begun","A criterion is not met, because a screening programme should be a once and for all project"],"e":"The criteria include that the condition should be an important health problem and that there should be an accepted treatment for patients with recognised disease. Facilities for diagnosis and treatment should be available, and there should be a suitable and acceptable test. A good test on its own is therefore not enough.","w":["A suitable test is only one of the ten criteria.","The criterion is that the condition is an important health problem, not that it is rare.","An accepted treatment is one of the criteria for considering a programme.","Case-finding should be continuous, not a \"once and for all\" project."],"k":"Wilson-Jungner: an accepted treatment must exist."},{"id":46020,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Which option is <b>not</b> one of the Wilson-Jungner criteria?","o":["The test must give a definite diagnosis in every person tested","The condition should be an important health problem","There should be a recognisable latent or early symptomatic phase","The test should be acceptable to the population","Case-finding should be a continuous process"],"e":"The ten criteria cover an important condition, accepted treatment, available facilities, a latent or early symptomatic phase, a suitable test, acceptability, understood natural history, an agreed treatment policy, balanced cost and continuous case-finding. Screening tests are not intended to be diagnostic, so a definite diagnosis in everyone is not a criterion.","w":["An important health problem is criterion 1.","A recognisable latent or early symptomatic phase is criterion 4.","Acceptability to the population is criterion 6.","Continuous case-finding is criterion 10."],"k":"Wilson-Jungner does not require a screening test to be diagnostic.","rn":[["","Screening vs diagnostic: meaning of a positive result","Screening vs diagnostic: meaning of a positive result","Screening: suspicion of disease needing confirmation; Diagnostic: definite diagnosis"],["","Diagnostic tests give more weight to what than patient acceptability?","Diagnostic tests give more weight to what than patient acceptability?","Accuracy; Precision"],["","What is population-based screening?","What is population-based screening?","Systematically screens every person in a population"]]},{"id":46021,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"A condition has no silent phase: disease is immediately severe from the moment it starts. Which Wilson-Jungner criterion is most clearly failed?","o":["A recognisable latent or early symptomatic phase","An agreed policy on whom to treat as patients","An economically balanced cost of case-finding","Facilities for diagnosis and treatment","Case-finding as a continuous process"],"e":"Criterion 4 requires a recognisable latent or early symptomatic phase, which is the window in which to catch a condition early. Without this, there is nothing for screening to find before severe disease. The other criteria concern treatment policy, cost, facilities and continuity.","w":["The scenario says nothing about who should be treated.","The scenario gives no cost information.","The scenario does not mention the availability of facilities.","The scenario does not concern whether the programme is ongoing."],"k":"Screening needs a latent or early window to catch the condition.","rn":[["","What does early detection imply?","What does early detection imply?","Pathological changes underway and detectable; Not yet symptomatic"]]},{"id":46022,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"A programme tests everyone once in a single year, then stops permanently. Which criterion does this most clearly breach?","a":["case-finding should be continuous","continuous case-finding","continuous process","case finding should be a continuous process","case-finding should be a continuous process","continuous","criterion 10"],"e":"Criterion 10 states that case-finding should be a continuous process and not a \"once and for all\" project. A single round misses the people who become eligible or develop the condition afterwards. A programme must keep going.","k":"Case-finding must be continuous, not once and for all."},{"id":46023,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"A well-validated test finds a condition early, but most people refuse it because it is very unpleasant. Which criterion is most clearly failed?","o":["The test should be acceptable to the population","The natural history should be adequately understood","The condition should be an important health problem","The cost should be balanced with medical care as a whole","There should be an accepted treatment for recognised disease"],"e":"Criterion 6 states that the test should be acceptable to the population, which means people will agree to have it. Screening tests should be simple, acceptable to patients and staff, and not harmful. A test that people refuse cannot sort the population effectively.","w":["The natural history is not the issue described.","The importance of the condition is not the issue described.","The cost is not the issue described.","The treatment is not the issue described."],"k":"A screening test must be acceptable to the population.","rn":[["","Screening vs diagnostic: test method","Screening vs diagnostic: test method","Screening: simple, acceptable, not harmful; Diagnostic: maybe invasive, expensive"]]},{"id":46024,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A proposed programme would find many early lesions, but nobody knows which would ever progress to declared disease. Which criterion is unmet?","o":["The natural history of the condition should be adequately understood","The test should be acceptable to the people being screened","Facilities for diagnosis and treatment should be available","Case-finding should be a continuous process","The test should be cheap for the population"],"e":"Criterion 7 requires the natural history of the condition, including development from latent to declared disease, to be adequately understood. If nobody knows how lesions progress, then it is unclear what early detection achieves. Criterion 8 on whom to treat also becomes harder to define.","w":["Acceptability is not the problem in this scenario.","The availability of facilities is not the problem in this scenario.","Continuity is not the problem in this scenario.","Test cost is not the problem in this scenario."],"k":"Natural history from latent to declared disease must be understood.","rn":[["","The types of prevention mirror what?","The types of prevention mirror what?","Natural history of disease"]]},{"id":46025,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"A person notices a new symptom, sees a doctor and is rapidly investigated. Another person has no symptoms and is invited for a test. Which option correctly labels them?","o":["The first is early diagnosis; the second is screening","The first is screening; the second is early diagnosis","Both are screening, because both are tested quickly","Both are early diagnosis, because both come to clinic","The first is primordial; the second is tertiary"],"e":"The dividing line is symptom onset. Screening occurs before symptom onset, is a service provided to a healthy population, and looks for asymptomatic disease. Early diagnosis occurs after symptom onset, and is a service provided only for people with symptoms.","w":["This reverses the two labels.","A person with symptoms is outside the definition of screening.","A person with no symptoms is outside the definition of early diagnosis.","Neither of these is a population policy or a rehabilitation measure."],"k":"Symptom onset divides screening from early diagnosis.","rn":[["","How does breast cancer screening reduce mortality?","How does breast cancer screening reduce mortality?","Early detection and early treatment; Of asymptomatic cancers"],["","How does screening reduce mortality?","How does screening reduce mortality?","Early detection; Early treatment"],["","What is the purpose of screening?","What is the purpose of screening?","Identify higher-risk people in an apparently healthy population; Offer early treatment or intervention"]]},{"id":46026,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Which row correctly compares a screening test with a diagnostic test?","o":["Screening is simple and cheap; diagnostic tests may be invasive and expensive but justified","Screening is invasive and expensive; diagnostic tests are simple, cheap and always harmless","Screening gives a definite diagnosis; diagnostic tests only raise suspicion of a disease","Screening targets only symptomatic people; diagnostic tests target only asymptomatic people","Screening favours patient acceptance less; diagnostic tests favour it above accuracy"],"e":"Screening tests are simple, acceptable to patients and staff, should not be harmful, and are cheap because large numbers are screened. Diagnostic tests may be invasive and expensive, but this is justifiable to establish a diagnosis. A positive screening result only indicates suspicion, while a diagnostic result provides a definite diagnosis.","w":["This reverses the method and the cost of the two kinds of test.","This reverses what each kind of test gives.","Screening targets asymptomatic people, and diagnostic tests include symptomatic people.","Diagnostic tests give more weight to accuracy and precision than to acceptability."],"k":"Screening: simple, cheap, safe. Diagnostic: may be invasive, costly, definitive.","rn":[["","Screening vs diagnostic: test method","Screening vs diagnostic: test method","Screening: simple, acceptable, not harmful; Diagnostic: maybe invasive, expensive"]]},{"id":46027,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Why is the threshold for a positive screening test generally set towards high sensitivity?","o":["So that potential disease is not missed","So that every positive result proves disease","So that all false positives are removed","So that the gold standard is no longer needed","So that fewer people will be sent for diagnostic follow-up"],"e":"The screening threshold is generally chosen towards high sensitivity, in order not to miss potential disease. This accepts more false positives, which is acceptable because a positive result only warrants confirmation. Diagnostic tests are chosen towards high specificity, with more weight on accuracy and precision.","w":["A positive screening result indicates only suspicion of disease.","A sensitive threshold tends to allow more false positives rather than removing them.","Positive screening results still need the gold standard.","A sensitive threshold tends to send more people for follow-up, not fewer."],"k":"Screening thresholds favour sensitivity, so as not to miss disease.","rn":[["","Why is a screening threshold chosen towards high sensitivity?","Why is a screening threshold chosen towards high sensitivity?","Not to miss potential disease"]]},{"id":46028,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"A patient with a lump has a biopsy to determine definitively whether cancer is present. Which test property is favoured here?","a":["specificity","high specificity"],"e":"Diagnostic tests lean towards high specificity, as with a biopsy of a lump where an accurate diagnosis is needed. They give more weight to accuracy and precision than to patient acceptability. Screening tests instead lean towards high sensitivity.","k":"Diagnostic tests lean to high specificity.","rn":[["","Why does a diagnostic test favour high specificity?","Why does a diagnostic test favour high specificity?","More accuracy on diagnosis; E.g. a biopsy of a lump must detect cancer cells accurately"]]},{"id":46029,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Which group is <b>not</b> an appropriate target for a diagnostic test?","o":["A large asymptomatic population with no prior test result","A patient with symptoms who needs a diagnosis established","An asymptomatic person with a positive screening result","A patient with a lump who needs an accurate diagnosis","A person with suspicious findings referred after screening"],"e":"Diagnostic tests target symptomatic individuals to establish a diagnosis, or asymptomatic individuals with a positive screening test. Large numbers of asymptomatic but potentially at-risk individuals are the target of screening tests, which are simple and cheap. Expensive and possibly invasive tests would not be justified for them.","w":["Symptomatic patients are a target of diagnostic testing.","Asymptomatic people with a positive screen are a target of diagnostic testing.","A patient with a lump needs an accurate diagnosis, as in a biopsy.","People with suspicious findings must be referred for diagnosis."],"k":"Diagnostic tests: symptomatic people or positive screens, not whole populations.","rn":[["","Screening vs diagnostic: target population","Screening vs diagnostic: target population","Screening: large numbers, asymptomatic, potentially at risk; Diagnostic: symptomatic, or positive screening test"]]},{"id":46030,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"A screening test costs a small amount per person but must be given to very many people to find a few potential cases. What is the key cost principle?","o":["The test must be cheap, and its benefits should justify the costs","The test may be very expensive, as cost never matters in screening","The test must be free of false results before any costs can be justified","The test must cost more than the gold standard to be taken seriously","The test must be funded only by the people who test positive on it"],"e":"Screening tests are cheap, and benefits should justify the costs, since large numbers of people will need to be screened to identify a small number of potential cases. Higher costs may be justified for a diagnostic test to establish a diagnosis. Some programmes have small mortality benefits, so public money is a real issue.","w":["The cost of case-finding should be economically balanced.","No test is 100% accurate, so this condition could not be met.","Screening tests should be cheap, unlike diagnostic tests.","Funding by those who test positive is not part of the principle."],"k":"Screening is cheap per person because many must be tested.","rn":[["","Why must a screening test be cheap?","Why must a screening test be cheap?","Large numbers screened; Few potential cases identified"],["","Screening vs diagnostic: cost","Screening vs diagnostic: cost","Screening: cheap; Diagnostic: higher, may be justified"]]},{"id":46031,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"Cervical screening identifies and treats precancerous stages. Which aim is <b>most</b> specifically served by treating these precursors?","o":["Reducing incidence","Increasing choice","Reducing severity","Raising prevalence","Reducing specificity"],"e":"The four aims are to reduce mortality, reduce incidence, reduce severity and increase choice. Incidence is reduced by identifying and treating precursors. Cervical cancer screening aims to reduce the incidence and mortality of cervical cancer by identifying and treating precancerous stages.","w":["Increasing choice is the aim illustrated by antenatal screening.","Reducing severity is the aim illustrated by diabetic retinopathy screening.","Raising prevalence is not an aim of screening.","Reducing specificity is not an aim of screening."],"k":"Treating precursors reduces incidence (cervical screening).","rn":[["","How does cervical cancer screening reduce incidence and mortality?","How does cervical cancer screening reduce incidence and mortality?","Identifying and treating precancerous stages"],["","How does screening reduce the incidence of a condition?","How does screening reduce the incidence of a condition?","Identifying and treating its precursors"],["","Which 2 aims does cervical cancer screening serve?","Which 2 aims does cervical cancer screening serve?","Reduce incidence; Reduce mortality"]]},{"id":46032,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"Match the programme to its illustrated aim. Which pairing is correct?","o":["Diabetic retinopathy screening: reducing severity to prevent blindness","Breast screening: increasing parents' choice about a pregnancy","Antenatal screening: reducing mortality from asymptomatic cancers","Cervical screening: reducing severity of diabetic eye disease","Breast screening: treating fetal conditions in pregnancy"],"e":"Breast cancer screening illustrates reducing mortality through early detection and treatment of asymptomatic cancers. Cervical screening illustrates reducing incidence. Diabetic retinopathy screening illustrates reducing severity, by early detection and treatment to prevent blindness. Antenatal screening illustrates increasing choice.","w":["Breast screening illustrates reducing mortality.","Antenatal screening illustrates increasing choice.","Cervical screening illustrates reducing incidence and has nothing to do with the eye.","Breast screening illustrates reducing mortality from cancer."],"k":"Breast: mortality. Cervical: incidence. Retinopathy: severity. Antenatal: choice.","rn":[["","Image card: Which screening programme uses this eye image? What is its aim?","Which screening programme uses this eye image? What is its aim?","Diabetic retinopathy screening; Reduce severity, prevent blindness"],["","Diabetic retinopathy screening aims to prevent what?","Diabetic retinopathy screening aims to prevent what?","Blindness"]]},{"id":46033,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"Antenatal screening detects a condition in a fetus, and information is given to the parents. Which aim of screening does this best illustrate?","a":["increase choice","increasing choice","choice","informed choice","to increase choice"],"e":"Increasing choice means identifying conditions or risk factors at an early stage in a life-course, when more options are available. One aim of antenatal screening is to detect conditions in the fetus and provide information to parents, so they can make an informed choice about whether to continue or end a pregnancy.","k":"Antenatal screening increases informed choice.","rn":[["","How does screening increase choice?","How does screening increase choice?","Identifies conditions or risk factors early in the life-course; More options available"],["","What does antenatal screening do for parents?","What does antenatal screening do for parents?","Detects conditions in the fetus; Gives information for informed choice"]]},{"id":46034,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"What does \"early detection\" imply in the aim of reducing mortality?","o":["Pathological changes are under way and detectable, but not yet symptomatic","Pathological changes have not begun, but risk factors have been removed","Symptoms have just begun, and the person has presented to their doctor","The outcome has already been reached, and rehabilitation is about to start","The disease has spread, but the patient has not yet received a definite diagnosis"],"e":"Early detection implies that pathological changes are under way and detectable, but not yet symptomatic. This corresponds to the subclinical stage. The aim is to reduce mortality through early detection and early treatment of a condition.","w":["With no pathological changes, there is nothing yet to detect.","Once symptoms are present, this is early diagnosis rather than screening.","Rehabilitation belongs to tertiary prevention.","Spread of disease is not implied by the definition."],"k":"Early detection: detectable changes, no symptoms yet.","rn":[["","What does early detection imply?","What does early detection imply?","Pathological changes underway and detectable; Not yet symptomatic"]]},{"id":46035,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"Which sequence correctly describes a screening programme?","o":["Identify eligible people, invite and inform, test, refer and report, diagnose, treat, report outcomes","Invite and inform, test, identify eligible people, diagnose, refer and report, report outcomes, treat","Test, diagnose, identify eligible people, treat, invite and inform, report outcomes, refer and report","Identify eligible people, test, treat, invite and inform, diagnose, refer and report, report outcomes","Report outcomes, identify eligible people, diagnose, test, invite and inform, treat, refer and report"],"e":"A programme identifies the eligible population, then invites the full cohort with information. It conducts tests using agreed methods, refers screen positives and reports screen negatives. True cases are then diagnosed, cases are treated and followed up, and outcomes are reported to improve the programme.","w":["The eligible population must be identified before invitation.","Testing cannot come before the population has been identified and invited.","Treatment cannot come before invitation and diagnosis.","Outcomes are reported at the end of the sequence."],"k":"Find, invite, test, refer, diagnose, treat, report.","rn":[["","Screening programme step 7: what does reporting of outcomes involve?","Screening programme step 7: what does reporting of outcomes involve?","Collect, analyse and report outcomes; To identify false negatives; To improve effectiveness and cost-effectiveness"],["","What 2 reasons are outcomes reported in screening programmes?","What 2 reasons are outcomes reported in screening programmes?","Identify false negatives; Improve effectiveness and cost-effectiveness"],["","What 2 things does the diagnosis step do?","What 2 things does the diagnosis step do?","Diagnose true cases; Identify false positives"]]},{"id":46036,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"At which step of a screening programme are false positives identified?","a":["diagnosis","diagnostic","diagnosis step","at diagnosis"],"e":"At the diagnosis step, true cases are diagnosed and false positives identified. Screen-positive people are referred to appropriate services for this. False negatives, on the other hand, are identified through the reporting of outcomes.","k":"Diagnosis step: confirms true cases, identifies false positives.","rn":[["","What 2 things does the diagnosis step do?","What 2 things does the diagnosis step do?","Diagnose true cases; Identify false positives"]]},{"id":46037,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"A screened person with a negative test later develops the disease. Which programme step is designed to identify such cases and improve the programme?","o":["Reporting of outcomes","Invitation and information","Identifying the eligible population","Diagnosis of screen positives","Intervention and treatment"],"e":"Reporting of outcomes involves collecting, analysing and reporting on outcomes to identify false negatives. It improves the effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of the programme, and also justifies having it. Diagnosis of screen positives cannot find false negatives, because those people tested negative.","w":["Invitation enables informed choice but does not find missed cases.","Identifying the eligible group comes before anyone has been tested.","Those with a negative test do not go to diagnosis through the screening route.","Intervention follows diagnosis and does not find missed cases."],"k":"Outcome reporting identifies false negatives.","rn":[["","Screening programme step 7: what does reporting of outcomes involve?","Screening programme step 7: what does reporting of outcomes involve?","Collect, analyse and report outcomes; To identify false negatives; To improve effectiveness and cost-effectiveness"],["","What 2 reasons are outcomes reported in screening programmes?","What 2 reasons are outcomes reported in screening programmes?","Identify false negatives; Improve effectiveness and cost-effectiveness"]]},{"id":46038,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"A programme is sending the same letter to everyone, and some groups do not understand it. Which requirement of the invitation stage is not being met?","o":["Information should be tailored to different groups to enable informed choice","Only people with a positive result should get information about the screening","Invitations should go to only a small part of the cohort for reasons of cost","Information should be withheld until after the test has been fully completed","Registers should be deleted as soon as the invitation letters have been sent"],"e":"At the invitation stage, the full cohort is invited, with information tailored appropriately for different groups to enable informed choice to participate. Registers help ensure that people's details are collected and up to date. How to ensure informed choice is also a key issue for screening.","w":["Information is for everyone invited, so that they can choose whether to take part.","The full cohort should be invited.","Information should enable a choice before testing.","Registers keep details collected and up to date."],"k":"Invite the full cohort with tailored information for informed choice.","rn":[["","Why is invitation information tailored for different groups?","Why is invitation information tailored for different groups?","To enable informed choice to participate"],["","Screening programme step 2: who is invited, and with what information?","Screening programme step 2: who is invited, and with what information?","The full cohort; Information tailored to different groups"]]},{"id":46039,"t":"Types of Screening","s":"During a clinic visit for another reason, a 22-year-old is offered a chlamydia test. Which type of screening is this?","o":["Opportunistic","Population-based","Multiphasic","Tertiary","Primordial"],"e":"Opportunistic screening takes place during routine healthcare visits. A chlamydia test in males and females under 25 years of age during a visit to a primary care clinic is the example given. The defining feature is that the test is added to a contact that was already happening.","w":["Population-based screening systematically screens every person within a population.","Multiphasic screening applies more than one test for more than one condition.","Tertiary is a level of prevention, not a type of screening.","Primordial is a level of prevention, not a type of screening."],"k":"Opportunistic = during routine healthcare visits.","rn":[["","What is opportunistic screening?","What is opportunistic screening?","During routine healthcare visits"]]},{"id":46040,"t":"Types of Screening","s":"A health check includes blood pressure, bloods, urine, and height and weight for body mass index. Which type of screening is this?","a":["multiphasic","multiphasic screening"],"e":"Multiphasic screening applies more than one test for more than one condition. The National Health Service health check, with blood pressure, bloods, urine, and height and weight for body mass index, is the example. It can pick up people with silent hypertension.","k":"Multiphasic = several tests for several conditions.","rn":[["","The NHS health check is an example of which type of screening?","The NHS health check is an example of which type of screening?","Multiphasic"],["","What is multiphasic screening?","What is multiphasic screening?","More than one test for more than one condition"]]},{"id":46041,"t":"Types of Screening","s":"A clinic screens only a group at high risk of a condition, using several tests for several different conditions at the same visit. How is this best described?","o":["Both selective and multiphasic, as the types are not mutually exclusive","Population-based only, as testing has been done on a group of people","Opportunistic only, as the people tested have all been to a clinic","Neither selective nor multiphasic, as the two types can never be combined","Early diagnosis only, as high-risk people are assumed to have symptoms"],"e":"Selective screening is applied to a particular subset of the population, such as a high-risk group. Multiphasic screening applies more than one test for more than one condition. These types are not necessarily mutually exclusive, so selective screening can be multiphasic.","w":["Population-based screening systematically covers every person within a population.","The defining feature here is a chosen group and several tests, not a routine visit.","The types can overlap.","High-risk people are not assumed to have symptoms."],"k":"Screening types can overlap: selective can be multiphasic.","rn":[["","Are the types of screening mutually exclusive?","Are the types of screening mutually exclusive?","No; E.g. selective can be multiphasic"]]},{"id":46042,"t":"Types of Screening","s":"Which type of screening systematically tests every person within a defined population?","a":["population-based","population based","population-based screening","population based screening","mass screening"],"e":"Population-based screening systematically screens every person within a population. The National Health Service programmes for abdominal aortic aneurysm, certain cancers, diabetic eye disease and antenatal and newborn conditions are examples. Selective screening instead targets a subset.","k":"Population-based = systematically everyone in the population.","rn":[["","What is population-based screening?","What is population-based screening?","Systematically screens every person in a population"]]},{"id":46043,"t":"Types of Screening","s":"A man is about to turn 65. Which National Health Service population screening programme is specifically offered to him in that year?","o":["Abdominal aortic aneurysm screening","Cervical cancer screening","Newborn blood spot screening","Sickle cell and thalassaemia","Fetal anomaly screening"],"e":"Abdominal aortic aneurysm screening is offered to men during the year they turn 65. Cervical screening, newborn blood spot and fetal anomaly screening clearly do not fit a man of this age. Sickle cell and thalassaemia screening is a separate programme.","w":["Cervical screening is a cancer programme and is not offered at this trigger.","Newborn blood spot screening is for newborn babies.","Sickle cell and thalassaemia is not defined by turning 65.","Fetal anomaly screening is part of antenatal screening."],"k":"AAA screening: men in the year they turn 65.","rn":[["","Who is offered AAA (abdominal aortic aneurysm) screening?","Who is offered AAA (abdominal aortic aneurysm) screening?","Men; During the year they turn 65"]]},{"id":46044,"t":"Types of Screening","s":"Which set contains the three cancers with National Health Service population-based screening?","o":["Bowel, breast and cervical","Breast, lung and prostate","Bowel, prostate and cervical","Skin, breast and cervical","Bowel, breast and pancreatic"],"e":"The National Health Service population-based cancer programmes are bowel, breast and cervical. Other programmes cover abdominal aortic aneurysm, antenatal and newborn conditions, diabetic eye disease, and sickle cell and thalassaemia. These are the programmes likely to be met on general practice placements.","w":["Lung and prostate are not in the listed cancer programmes.","Prostate is not in the listed cancer programmes.","Skin is not in the listed cancer programmes.","Pancreatic is not in the listed cancer programmes."],"k":"NHS cancer screening: bowel, breast, cervical.","rn":[["","What are the 3 NHS cancer screening programmes?","What are the 3 NHS cancer screening programmes?","Bowel; Breast; Cervical"]]},{"id":46045,"t":"Types of Screening","s":"Which option is <b>not</b> part of the antenatal and newborn population screening programmes?","o":["Newborn abdominal aortic aneurysm scan","Newborn hearing","Newborn blood spot","Fetal anomaly","Infectious diseases in pregnancy"],"e":"Antenatal and newborn programmes are fetal anomaly, infectious diseases in pregnancy, newborn and infant physical examination, newborn blood spot and newborn hearing. Abdominal aortic aneurysm screening is offered to men during the year they turn 65, not to newborn babies.","w":["Newborn hearing is a listed programme.","Newborn blood spot is a listed programme.","Fetal anomaly is a listed programme.","Infectious diseases in pregnancy is a listed programme."],"k":"Antenatal/newborn: fetal anomaly, infections, physical exam, blood spot, hearing.","rn":[["","What are the 5 groups of population-based screening in the NHS?","What are the 5 groups of population-based screening in the NHS?","AAA (abdominal aortic aneurysm); Antenatal and newborn; Cancers; Diabetic eye disease; Sickle cell and thalassemia"],["","Who is offered AAA (abdominal aortic aneurysm) screening?","Who is offered AAA (abdominal aortic aneurysm) screening?","Men; During the year they turn 65"]]},{"id":46046,"t":"Implementing Screening","s":"A programme has only a small benefit in terms of mortality. Which issue does this most directly raise?","o":["Whether it is justified against other calls on public money","Whether its positive results can be regarded as definite diagnoses","Whether it should move to the primordial level of prevention","Whether symptoms should be required before testing anyone","Whether the disease has any natural history at all"],"e":"Key issues regarding screening include whether programmes with small mortality benefits are justified against other calls on public money. Others are how to ensure people can make informed choices and how to manage public expectations. Screening has costs and downsides as well as benefits.","w":["Positive screening results are never definite diagnoses.","Primordial prevention refers to population policy before risk factors develop.","Requiring symptoms would mean it was no longer screening.","All diseases follow a natural history, so this is not the issue raised."],"k":"Small mortality benefit raises the question of public money.","rn":[["","What are the 3 key issues regarding screening?","What are the 3 key issues regarding screening?","Small mortality benefits vs public money; Ensuring informed choices; Managing public expectations"]]},{"id":46047,"t":"Implementing Screening","s":"Which statement most accurately summarises the overall message about the value of screening?","o":["It makes sense intuitively, but its benefits do not always outweigh the downsides","It always provides overall benefit because any earlier detection is an improvement","It never provides overall benefit, because every positive result is a false alarm","It is useful only for conditions in which the test is exactly 100% accurate","It has no downsides at all, as long as a person's test result comes back negative"],"e":"Screening for disease and risk of disease may make sense intuitively, but the benefits do not always outweigh the downsides. People therefore need honest information, and public expectations must be managed. No test is 100% accurate, and false positive results lead to follow-up.","w":["Benefits do not always outweigh the downsides.","Many positive results are true, and screening can improve outcomes for some.","No test is 100% accurate.","A false negative is possible, so a negative result still has a downside."],"k":"Screening: intuitive, but benefits do not always outweigh downsides.","rn":[["","What is the key take-home message about screening?","What is the key take-home message about screening?","Makes sense intuitively; Benefits do not always outweigh downsides"]]},{"id":46048,"t":"Implementing Screening","s":"Which organisation advises ministers in the four UK countries on implementing, continuing and modifying population-based screening programmes?","o":["The National Screening Committee","UK Health Security Agency","Public Health England","World Health Organisation","Department of Health and Social Care"],"e":"The National Screening Committee advises ministers in the four UK countries on implementing, continuing and modifying population-based screening programmes. It considers all the evidence and makes recommendations based on criteria that evolved from the Wilson-Jungner criteria. Recommendations are reviewed regularly.","w":["The UK Health Security Agency responds to external health threats.","Public Health England advises on implementing safe, high-quality programmes in England.","The World Health Organisation published the source definition and criteria.","The department sponsors an executive agency, and this advisory role is the committee's."],"k":"National Screening Committee advises ministers, using evolved Wilson-Jungner criteria.","rn":[["","What does the National Screening Committee base its recommendations on?","What does the National Screening Committee base its recommendations on?","All the evidence; Criteria that evolved from the Wilson-Jungner criteria"],["","Who advises ministers in the 4 UK countries on screening programmes?","Who advises ministers in the 4 UK countries on screening programmes?","National Screening Committee"],["","The National Screening Committee advises on which 3 things?","The National Screening Committee advises on which 3 things?","Implementing; Continuing; Modifying"]]},{"id":46049,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A woman's screening test is negative, but a later gold standard test shows that she has the disease. How is the screening result classified?","o":["False negative","False positive","True negative","True positive","Indeterminate"],"e":"In a 2 x 2 table, a false negative means the test said negative but the person has the disease, which is a missed case. A false positive is a false alarm, with a positive test and no disease. The gold standard defines the truth against which the test is compared.","w":["A false positive has a positive test with no disease.","A true negative has a negative test and no disease.","A true positive has a positive test and disease.","The result can be classified, because the gold standard gives the truth."],"k":"False negative: test negative, disease present (a missed case).","rn":[["","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","More true negatives per false negative; False negatives unlikely as disease is rare"]]},{"id":46050,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A person with no disease on biopsy had a positive screening test. What is this result called?","a":["false positive","fp","a false positive","false-positive"],"e":"A false positive means the test said positive, but the person has no disease. It is a false alarm. A low positive predictive value means that a lot of resource is used on diagnostic follow-up of false-positive patients.","k":"False positive: test positive, no disease (false alarm).","rn":[["","Low prevalence: what might a positive result be due to?","Low prevalence: what might a positive result be due to?","A false positive"],["","What does a low PPV (positive predictive value) imply?","What does a low PPV (positive predictive value) imply?","Much resource spent following up false positives"]]},{"id":46051,"t":"Worked Examples","s":"A test is applied to 200 people with a disease, confirmed by gold standard. It gives 160 positives and 40 negatives in this group. What is its sensitivity?","o":["80%","20%","25%","40%","160%"],"e":"Sensitivity is true positives divided by true positives plus false negatives. Here that is 160 / (160 + 40) = 160 / 200 = 80%. It reads down the \"has disease\" column only. People without the disease do not enter this calculation at all.","w":["This is the proportion missed, 40 / 200, which is the false negative share.","This divides 40 by 160, which is not a measure defined here.","This treats the number of false negatives as a percentage.","A proportion of the diseased group cannot be more than 100%."],"k":"Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN).","rn":[["","Heart failure test: TP 20, FN 5. What is the sensitivity?","Heart failure test: TP 20, FN 5. What is the sensitivity?","80%"],["","What is the formula for sensitivity?","What is the formula for sensitivity?","TP / (TP + FN)"],["","H. pylori breath test: TP 40, FN 60. Sensitivity?","H. pylori breath test: TP 40, FN 60. Sensitivity?","40%"]]},{"id":46052,"t":"Test Performance","s":"A test is applied to 500 people confirmed to have no disease. It gives 50 positives and 450 negatives in this group. What is its specificity?","o":["90%","10%","45%","50%","11.1%"],"e":"Specificity is true negatives divided by false positives plus true negatives. Here that is 450 / (50 + 450) = 450 / 500 = 90%. It reads down the \"no disease\" column only. People with the disease do not enter this calculation.","w":["This is the false positive share of this group, 50 / 500.","This is not the result of dividing true negatives by all people without disease.","This treats the number of false positives as a percentage.","This divides 50 by 450, which uses the wrong denominator."],"k":"Specificity = TN / (FP + TN).","rn":[["","What is the formula for specificity?","What is the formula for specificity?","TN / (TN + FP)"],["","H. pylori breath test: TN 60, FP 60. Specificity?","H. pylori breath test: TN 60, FP 60. Specificity?","50%"]]},{"id":46053,"t":"Test Performance","s":"A test has very high sensitivity. A patient's result is negative. What is the most appropriate conclusion?","o":["There is a high degree of certainty that the patient does not have the disease","There is a high degree of certainty that the patient has the disease","The result is worthless, as high sensitivity tests give only false negatives","The patient may or may not have the disease, as a negative result gives no information","The patient must have a biopsy, because a negative result can never be trusted"],"e":"High sensitivity means most people with the condition have been identified. So if the test is negative, there is a high degree of certainty that the subject does not have the disease. If it is positive, the subject may or may not have the disease, because many without it may test positive. Tests are not taken alone, however, so symptoms still need assessing.","w":["A negative result on a sensitive test points away from disease.","High sensitivity means that false negatives are few.","This describes a negative result on a highly specific test.","A negative result on a highly sensitive test carries useful reassurance."],"k":"High sensitivity: a negative result gives high certainty of no disease.","rn":[["","High sensitivity: what does a negative test mean?","High sensitivity: what does a negative test mean?","High certainty of no disease"],["","High specificity: what does a positive test mean?","High specificity: what does a positive test mean?","High certainty of disease"]]},{"id":46054,"t":"Test Performance","s":"A test has very high specificity. A patient's result is positive. What is the most appropriate conclusion?","o":["There is a high degree of certainty that the patient has the disease","There is a high degree of certainty that the patient does not have the disease","Many patients without the disease will have tested positive on this same test","The patient may or may not have the disease, as positives are mostly false here","The prevalence of the disease must be above fifty per cent in this population"],"e":"High specificity means most subjects with a positive test do have the disease. If the test is positive, there is a high degree of certainty that the subject has the disease. If it is negative, the subject may or may not have the disease. This is the opposite pattern to a highly sensitive test.","w":["A positive result on a specific test points towards disease.","High specificity means that false positives are few.","This describes a positive result on a highly sensitive test.","Specificity does not tell you the prevalence of the disease."],"k":"High specificity: a positive result gives high certainty of disease.","rn":[["","High specificity: what does a positive test mean?","High specificity: what does a positive test mean?","High certainty of disease"],["","High sensitivity: what does a negative test mean?","High sensitivity: what does a negative test mean?","High certainty of no disease"]]},{"id":46055,"t":"Test Performance","s":"In a study of 1000 people, a test gives 190 true positives, 160 false positives, 10 false negatives and 640 true negatives. What is the accuracy?","o":["83%","95%","80%","54%","98.5%"],"e":"Accuracy is the proportion of true positives and true negatives correctly identified: (TP + TN) / (TP + FP + FN + TN). Here that is (190 + 640) / 1000 = 830 / 1000 = 83%. Sensitivity reads 95%, specificity 80%, and positive predictive value 54%, which are different measures from the same table.","w":["This is the sensitivity, 190 / 200.","This is the specificity, 640 / 800.","This is the positive predictive value, 190 / 350.","This is the negative predictive value, 640 / 650."],"k":"Accuracy = (TP + TN) / everyone.","rn":[["","What is accuracy?","What is accuracy?","Proportion of true positives and true negatives correctly identified"],["","What is the formula for accuracy?","What is the formula for accuracy?","(TP + TN) / (TP + FP + FN + TN)"]]},{"id":46056,"t":"Test Performance","s":"Which measure of a test uses the whole 2 x 2 table, by dividing all correct results by everyone tested?","a":["accuracy"],"e":"Accuracy is the proportion of true positives and true negatives correctly identified, calculated as (TP + TN) / (TP + FP + FN + TN). Sensitivity reads only the \"has disease\" column, and specificity reads only the \"no disease\" column. Predictive values read across the rows.","k":"Accuracy uses the whole table.","rn":[["","What is accuracy?","What is accuracy?","Proportion of true positives and true negatives correctly identified"],["","What is the formula for accuracy?","What is the formula for accuracy?","(TP + TN) / (TP + FP + FN + TN)"]]},{"id":46057,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A patient has just had a positive test and asks, \"What is the chance I actually have this disease?\" Which measure answers that question?","o":["Positive predictive value","Negative predictive value","Specificity","Sensitivity","Accuracy"],"e":"Positive predictive value is the probability that a person has the condition if the result is positive. Sensitivity and specificity do not tell a patient what their own result means, whereas predictive values do. It is calculated as TP / (TP + FP) and is influenced by the test and by disease prevalence.","w":["Negative predictive value concerns a negative result.","Specificity concerns people without the condition.","Sensitivity concerns people with the condition.","Accuracy describes all correct results across the table."],"k":"PPV: given a positive test, the probability of disease.","rn":[["","What is PPV (positive predictive value)?","What is PPV (positive predictive value)?","Probability of having the condition given a positive test"],["","What is the formula for PPV (positive predictive value)?","What is the formula for PPV (positive predictive value)?","TP / (TP + FP)"],["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"]]},{"id":46058,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"In a table, there are 40 true positives, 45 false positives, 10 false negatives and 405 true negatives. What is the positive predictive value, to the nearest percent?","o":["47%","80%","90%","98%","88.9%"],"e":"Positive predictive value is TP / (TP + FP) = 40 / (40 + 45) = 40 / 85, which is about 47%. It reads across the positive row of the table. Sensitivity from this same table is 80%, specificity is 90%, and negative predictive value is about 98%.","w":["This is the sensitivity, 40 / 50.","This is the specificity, 405 / 450.","This is the negative predictive value, 405 / 415.","This divides 40 by 45, which does not use the sum of the positive row as the denominator."],"k":"PPV = TP / (TP + FP).","rn":[["","What is the formula for PPV (positive predictive value)?","What is the formula for PPV (positive predictive value)?","TP / (TP + FP)"]]},{"id":46059,"t":"Worked Examples","s":"In a table, there are 60 true positives, 32 false positives, 20 false negatives and 288 true negatives. What is the negative predictive value, to the nearest percent?","o":["94%","75%","90%","65%","87%"],"e":"Negative predictive value is TN / (FN + TN) = 288 / (20 + 288) = 288 / 308, which is about 94%. It reads across the negative row of the table. Sensitivity is 75%, specificity 90%, positive predictive value about 65%, and accuracy 87%.","w":["This is the sensitivity, 60 / 80.","This is the specificity, 288 / 320.","This is the positive predictive value, 60 / 92.","This is the accuracy, 348 / 400."],"k":"NPV = TN / (FN + TN).","rn":[["","Heart failure test: TN 73, FN 5. What is the NPV (negative predictive value)?","Heart failure test: TN 73, FN 5. What is the NPV (negative predictive value)?","94%"],["","What is the formula for NPV (negative predictive value)?","What is the formula for NPV (negative predictive value)?","TN / (TN + FN)"]]},{"id":46060,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"Name the measure that gives the probability that a person is disease free if their result is negative. Give the name or its abbreviation.","a":["negative predictive value","npv"],"e":"Negative predictive value (NPV) gives the probability that a person is disease free if their result is negative. It is calculated as TN / (FN + TN). The lower the prevalence, the higher the negative predictive value, because false negatives are unlikely when a disease is rare.","k":"NPV: given a negative test, the probability of being disease free.","rn":[["","What is NPV (negative predictive value)?","What is NPV (negative predictive value)?","Probability of being disease free given a negative test"]]},{"id":46061,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A test with 90% sensitivity and 90% specificity is used on 10,000 people, of whom 100 have the disease. What is the positive predictive value, to the nearest percent?","o":["8%","90%","50%","99%","1%"],"e":"Of 100 with disease, 90 are true positives and 10 are false negatives. Of 9,900 without disease, 8,910 are true negatives and 990 are false positives. Positive predictive value is 90 / (90 + 990) = 90 / 1,080, about 8%. With a rare disease, most positive results are false, even with a good test.","w":["This is the sensitivity or specificity, not the positive predictive value.","This would need equal numbers of true and false positives.","This is close to the negative predictive value, 8,910 / 8,920.","This is the prevalence of the disease."],"k":"Rare disease: PPV is low even with a good test.","rn":[["","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","More true negatives per false negative; False negatives unlikely as disease is rare"],["","Where is a negative COVID-19 (coronavirus disease 2019) antigen test more likely to be …","Where is a negative COVID-19 (coronavirus disease 2019) antigen test more likely to be a true negative?","Where the disease is rare"],["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"]]},{"id":46062,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A test with 90% sensitivity and 90% specificity is used on 1,000 people, of whom 500 have the disease. What is the positive predictive value?","o":["90%","50%","8%","99.9%","45%"],"e":"Of 500 with disease, 450 are true positives. Of 500 without disease, 50 are false positives. Positive predictive value is 450 / (450 + 50) = 90%. With a prevalence of 1%, the same test would give a positive predictive value of only about 8%, so the difference comes entirely from prevalence.","w":["This is the prevalence and not the positive predictive value.","This would fit a prevalence of 1%, not 50%.","A value this high would need far fewer false positives.","This is the share of everyone tested who are true positives, 450 / 1,000."],"k":"Same test, higher prevalence → higher PPV.","rn":[["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"],["","The lower the prevalence, what happens to NPV (negative predictive value)?","The lower the prevalence, what happens to NPV (negative predictive value)?","Higher"],["","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"]]},{"id":46063,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"As the prevalence of a disease rises in the population tested, what happens to the predictive values of a given test?","o":["Positive predictive value rises and negative predictive value falls","Positive predictive value falls and negative predictive value rises","Both predictive values rise together with the prevalence","Both predictive values fall together with the prevalence","Neither predictive value changes, as they depend only on the test itself"],"e":"As prevalence increases, positive predictive value increases, but negative predictive value decreases. Predictive values are influenced by both the sensitivity and specificity of the test and the disease prevalence. The two lines on the graph cross near a prevalence of 50.","w":["This is the pattern seen as prevalence falls.","The two values move in opposite directions.","The two values move in opposite directions.","Predictive values are influenced by prevalence as well as by the test."],"k":"Prevalence up: PPV up, NPV down.","rn":[["","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","More true negatives per false negative; False negatives unlikely as disease is rare"],["","Image card: What do the blue and red curves show? What do the green and purple lines mark?","What do the blue and red curves show? What do the green and purple lines mark?","Blue: PPV (positive predictive value); Red: NPV (negative predictive value); Green: prevalence of 6%; Purple: prevalence of 30%"],["","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"]]},{"id":46064,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A negative result on a test is received in a low-prevalence setting. Why is it especially reassuring?","o":["False negatives are unlikely because the disease is rare, so there are more true negatives for each","False negatives are common because the disease is rare, which makes the result easier to interpret","Sensitivity is always 100% in a low-prevalence setting, so that no case can ever be missed at all","Specificity is always 0% in a low-prevalence setting, so positive results are not possible there","Prevalence has no effect on a negative result, so any test gives the same degree of reassurance"],"e":"With low prevalence, there will be more true negatives for every false negative. A false negative means the person has the disease, which is unlikely because the disease is rare. So a negative result in a low-prevalence setting confidently rules out the disease. The lower the prevalence, the higher the negative predictive value.","w":["False negatives become less common rather than more common when disease is rare.","Sensitivity is a property of the test and does not become 100%.","Specificity does not become 0%, and positive results still occur.","Prevalence does influence the negative predictive value."],"k":"Rare disease: negative result is highly reassuring.","rn":[["","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","More true negatives per false negative; False negatives unlikely as disease is rare"]]},{"id":46065,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A person has a positive antigen test for COVID-19. In which setting is the positive result most likely to be a true positive?","o":["Where COVID-19 is common","Where COVID-19 is rare","Where nobody has symptoms and no COVID-19 has been reported in the area","The setting makes no difference","Only where the test is new"],"e":"With a positive antigen test, you are more likely to have COVID-19 if you live where it is common than where it is rare. Positive predictive value rises with prevalence. Conversely, with a negative antigen test, you are more likely to be COVID-free where it is rare.","w":["Low prevalence makes a positive result more likely to be false.","With no reported disease, prevalence is low, so positive results are more likely to be false.","Prevalence influences the predictive values.","Newness of a test is not the factor that affects this."],"k":"Positive test: more believable where disease is common.","rn":[["","Where is a positive COVID-19 (coronavirus disease 2019) antigen test more likely to be …","Where is a positive COVID-19 (coronavirus disease 2019) antigen test more likely to be a true positive?","Where the disease is common"]]},{"id":46066,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"On a graph of predictive values against prevalence, a line marks a prevalence of 6%, with one curve at about 25 and the other at about 99. What do these values mean?","o":["Positive predictive value is about 25%, and negative predictive value is about 99%","Positive predictive value is about 99%, and negative predictive value is about 25% at this prevalence","Sensitivity is about 25%, and specificity is about 99%","Sensitivity is about 99%, and specificity is about 25%","Accuracy is about 25%, and prevalence is about 99%"],"e":"At a prevalence of 6%, positive predictive value reads about 25 and negative predictive value about 99. At 30%, positive predictive value reads about 70 and negative predictive value about 93. So at low prevalence, a negative result is very reliable, while a positive may well be false.","w":["The low-prevalence pattern is high negative predictive value and low positive predictive value.","The graph plots predictive values and not sensitivity and specificity.","The graph plots predictive values and not sensitivity and specificity.","The prevalence here is 6%, and accuracy is not plotted."],"k":"Low prevalence: PPV low, NPV high.","rn":[["","Image card: What do the blue and red curves show? What do the green and purple lines mark?","What do the blue and red curves show? What do the green and purple lines mark?","Blue: PPV (positive predictive value); Red: NPV (negative predictive value); Green: prevalence of 6%; Purple: prevalence of 30%"],["","PSA (prostate-specific antigen) test: TN 748, FN 2. What is the NPV (negative predictiv…","PSA (prostate-specific antigen) test: TN 748, FN 2. What is the NPV (negative predictive value)?","99.7%"],["","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","More true negatives per false negative; False negatives unlikely as disease is rare"]]},{"id":46067,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"On a graph of predictive values against prevalence for one test, how do the values change between a prevalence of 6% and 30%?","o":["Positive predictive value rises from about 25 to 70, and negative predictive value falls from about 99 to 93","Positive predictive value falls from about 70 to 25, and negative predictive value rises from about 93 to 99","Both values stay exactly where they are, because the same test was used at both of these levels","Both values fall to about 50, because the lines cross at a prevalence of about 6%","Positive predictive value rises to 99, and negative predictive value falls to about 25"],"e":"At 6% prevalence, the blue line reads about 25 and the red about 99. At 30% prevalence, blue reads about 70 and red about 93. The lines cross near a prevalence of 50. Higher prevalence therefore improves the positive predictive value but lowers the negative predictive value.","w":["This is the direction of change as prevalence falls.","Prevalence changes the predictive values even when the test is the same.","The lines cross near a prevalence of 50, not 6%.","These figures are not the readings given at 30%."],"k":"PPV about 25 → 70, NPV about 99 → 93 from 6% to 30% prevalence.","rn":[["","Image card: What do the blue and red curves show? What do the green and purple lines mark?","What do the blue and red curves show? What do the green and purple lines mark?","Blue: PPV (positive predictive value); Red: NPV (negative predictive value); Green: prevalence of 6%; Purple: prevalence of 30%"],["","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","More true negatives per false negative; False negatives unlikely as disease is rare"]]},{"id":46068,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"Using sensitivity 0.8, specificity 0.9 and prevalence 0.1, calculate the positive predictive value from the prevalence formula.","o":["47%","80%","90%","97.6%","8%"],"e":"Positive predictive value = (sensitivity x prevalence) / [(sensitivity x prevalence) + ((1 - specificity) x (1 - prevalence))]. That is 0.08 / (0.08 + 0.09) = 0.08 / 0.17, which is about 47%. Negative predictive value here would be 0.81 / (0.81 + 0.02), about 98%.","w":["This is the sensitivity that was given.","This is the specificity that was given.","This is the negative predictive value for the same data.","This is the numerator alone, without dividing by all positives."],"k":"PPV formula combines sensitivity, specificity and prevalence.","rn":[["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"],["","PPV formula using sensitivity, specificity, prevalence","PPV formula using sensitivity, specificity, prevalence","(sens x prev) /; [(sens x prev) + (1-spec)(1-prev)]"],["","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"]]},{"id":46069,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"Which expression is the correct denominator in the formula for positive predictive value using prevalence?","o":["(sensitivity x prevalence) + ((1 - specificity) x (1 - prevalence))","(specificity x prevalence) + ((1 - sensitivity) x (1 - prevalence))","(specificity x (1 - prevalence)) + ((1 - sensitivity) x prevalence)","(sensitivity x (1 - prevalence)) + ((1 - specificity) x prevalence)","(sensitivity + specificity) x prevalence x (1 - prevalence) in the population"],"e":"The denominator represents all positive results: true positives (sensitivity x prevalence) plus false positives ((1 - specificity) x (1 - prevalence)). The negative predictive value denominator represents all negative results: true negatives plus false negatives.","w":["This swaps the roles of sensitivity and specificity.","This is the denominator for negative predictive value.","This pairs sensitivity with the people who do not have disease.","This expression is not part of the formula."],"k":"PPV denominator = all positives (TP share + FP share).","rn":[["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"],["","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"]]},{"id":46070,"t":"Test Performance","s":"A test has been applied to 300 people, of whom 60 have the disease. Of those with disease, 54 test positive. Specificity is 75%. How many false positives are there?","o":["60","6","54","180","240"],"e":"There are 300 - 60 = 240 people without disease. Specificity of 75% means that 180 are true negatives. The remaining 240 - 180 = 60 are false positives. False negatives are 60 - 54 = 6. Checking that each column adds to its total confirms the table.","w":["This is the number of false negatives.","This is the number of true positives.","This is the number of true negatives.","This is the total number without disease."],"k":"Fill missing cells from totals, then check columns."},{"id":46071,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A study finds 54 true positives, 6 false negatives, 60 false positives and 180 true negatives. What is the positive predictive value, to the nearest percent?","o":["47%","90%","75%","96.8%","20%"],"e":"Positive predictive value is 54 / (54 + 60) = 54 / 114, which is about 47%. It reads across the positive row. Sensitivity is 90%, specificity 75%, negative predictive value about 97%, and the prevalence is 20%.","w":["This is the sensitivity, 54 / 60.","This is the specificity, 180 / 240.","This is the negative predictive value, 180 / 186.","This is the prevalence, 60 / 300."],"k":"Read PPV across the positive row."},{"id":46072,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"A screening test has a sensitivity of 70%. It is used on 200 people who have the disease. How many cases will be missed?","a":["60","60 cases","sixty","60 people"],"e":"Sensitivity is TP / (TP + FN). With 70% sensitivity and 200 people with disease, there are 140 true positives. The remaining 60 are false negatives, or missed cases. This is why screening thresholds are chosen towards high sensitivity.","k":"Missed cases = (1 - sensitivity) x number with disease.","rn":[["","Why is a screening threshold chosen towards high sensitivity?","Why is a screening threshold chosen towards high sensitivity?","Not to miss potential disease"]]},{"id":46073,"t":"Worked Examples","s":"A test with 98% specificity is used on 5,000 people without the disease. How many false positives are expected?","a":["100","100 people","100 false positives","one hundred"],"e":"Specificity is TN / (FP + TN). With 98% specificity and 5,000 people without disease, 4,900 are true negatives and 100 are false positives. Even a high specificity leads to many false alarms when large numbers of well people are tested.","k":"False positives = (1 - specificity) x number without disease.","rn":[["","Heart failure test: 100 patients, 25 with disease. 20 test positive, 5 negative. Withou…","Heart failure test: 100 patients, 25 with disease. 20 test positive, 5 negative. Without disease, 2 test positive. How many true negatives?","73"]]},{"id":46074,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A programme screens 1,000 people, with a disease prevalence of 10%. The test has 80% sensitivity and 90% specificity. How many people will be referred for diagnostic follow-up as screen positives?","o":["170","80","90","100","830"],"e":"There are 100 with disease, giving 80 true positives. There are 900 without disease, giving 810 true negatives and 90 false positives. All screen positives are referred: 80 + 90 = 170. Only 80 of these truly have the disease.","w":["This counts only true positives, but false positives are referred as well.","This counts only false positives.","This is the number with disease, not the number of positive results.","This is the number of negative results, 810 + 20."],"k":"Referred = true positives + false positives."},{"id":46075,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A test has 20 true positives, 2 false positives, 5 false negatives and 73 true negatives. Which set of results is correct?","o":["Sensitivity 80%, specificity 97%, positive predictive value 91%, negative predictive value 94%","Sensitivity 97%, specificity 80%, positive predictive value 94%, negative predictive value 91%","Sensitivity 91%, specificity 94%, positive predictive value 80%, negative predictive value 97%","Sensitivity 80%, specificity 91%, positive predictive value 97%, negative predictive value 94%","Sensitivity 94%, specificity 97%, positive predictive value 91%, negative predictive value 80%"],"e":"Sensitivity = 20 / 25 = 80%. Specificity = 73 / 75 = 97%. Positive predictive value = 20 / 22 = 91%. Negative predictive value = 73 / 78 = 94%. Sensitivity and specificity read down the columns, and predictive values read across the rows.","w":["This swaps sensitivity with specificity and the two predictive values.","This swaps column-based and row-based measures.","This swaps specificity with positive predictive value.","This swaps sensitivity with negative predictive value."],"k":"Columns: sensitivity, specificity. Rows: predictive values.","rn":[["","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"],["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"]]},{"id":46076,"t":"Test Performance","s":"A blood marker test is compared with biopsy. There are 98 true positives, 152 false positives, 2 false negatives and 748 true negatives. What is the positive predictive value?","o":["39%","98%","83%","99.7%","25%"],"e":"Positive predictive value is 98 / (98 + 152) = 98 / 250 = 39%. So even with 98% sensitivity, only 39% of positives truly have cancer cells on biopsy. A negative result is very reassuring, at 99.7%. Sensitivity alone does not tell a patient what a positive result means.","w":["This is the sensitivity, 98 / 100.","This is the specificity, 748 / 900.","This is the negative predictive value, 748 / 750.","This is the share of all tested who are positive, 250 / 1,000."],"k":"High sensitivity does not mean a high PPV.","rn":[["","High sensitivity: what does a positive test mean?","High sensitivity: what does a positive test mean?","May or may not have disease"],["","High sensitivity: what does a negative test mean?","High sensitivity: what does a negative test mean?","High certainty of no disease"]]},{"id":46077,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A blood marker test compared with biopsy gives 98 true positives, 152 false positives, 2 false negatives and 748 true negatives. What is the best message to give a patient with a negative result?","o":["A negative result is very reassuring, as about 99.7% of negatives are free of cancer cells","A negative result means very little, as only 39% of negatives are free of cancer cells","A negative result proves absence of disease, as the test never produces false negatives","A negative result requires a biopsy in all cases, as the test has only 83% sensitivity","A negative result means the patient must be retested daily until it becomes positive"],"e":"Negative predictive value is 748 / (748 + 2) = 99.7%, so a negative result is very reassuring. It is not absolute, because there were 2 false negatives. Tests are not taken alone either, so any later symptoms still need assessing.","w":["The 39% figure is the positive predictive value.","There were 2 false negatives, so a negative result does not prove absence.","Sensitivity here is 98%, and the 83% figure is the specificity.","Repeated testing until positive is not appropriate."],"k":"NPV 99.7%: very reassuring, not absolute.","rn":[["","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","More true negatives per false negative; False negatives unlikely as disease is rare"],["","What is NPV (negative predictive value)?","What is NPV (negative predictive value)?","Probability of being disease free given a negative test"]]},{"id":46078,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"In a study, every one of 100 people tested positive, and 40 were confirmed by biopsy. What is the positive predictive value?","a":["40%","40","40 percent","40 per cent","0.4"],"e":"Positive predictive value is TP / (TP + FP) = 40 / (40 + 60) = 40%. There are 60 false positives. If everyone tested positive, there are no test-negative people, so no true negatives or false negatives can be counted from this group.","k":"PPV = TP / all positives.","rn":[["","What does a high PPV (positive predictive value) mean?","What does a high PPV (positive predictive value) mean?","Higher proportion of positives have the condition"],["","What does a low PPV (positive predictive value) imply?","What does a low PPV (positive predictive value) imply?","Much resource spent following up false positives"],["","What is accuracy?","What is accuracy?","Proportion of true positives and true negatives correctly identified"]]},{"id":46079,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"When drawing a 2 x 2 table for a test, which layout allows the measures to be read correctly?","o":["Test result down the side and truth across the top, reading sensitivity and specificity down columns","Truth down the side and test result across the top, reading sensitivity and specificity down columns","Test result down the side and truth across the top, reading predictive values down the columns","Test result down the side and truth across the top, reading sensitivity across the rows","Prevalence down the side and accuracy across the top, reading all measures diagonally"],"e":"Draw the table with test result down the side and truth across the top. Fill in the numbers given and work out any missing ones from the totals, checking that each column adds up. Read sensitivity and specificity down the columns, and predictive values across the rows.","w":["The layout is reversed, so reading down the columns would give predictive values.","Predictive values are read across the rows.","Sensitivity is read down the disease column.","Prevalence and accuracy are not the axes of the table."],"k":"Table: result on the side, truth on top. Columns then rows.","rn":[["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"],["","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"],["","NHS test sensitivity and specificity are always above what?","NHS test sensitivity and specificity are always above what?","80%"]]},{"id":46080,"t":"Test Performance","s":"Which formula is correct?","o":["Specificity = TN / (FP + TN)","Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FP)","Positive predictive value = TP / (TP + FN)","Negative predictive value = TN / (FP + TN)","Accuracy = (TP + FP) / (FN + TN)"],"e":"Specificity is TN / (FP + TN). Sensitivity is TP / (TP + FN). Positive predictive value is TP / (TP + FP). Negative predictive value is TN / (FN + TN). Accuracy is (TP + TN) / (TP + FP + FN + TN).","w":["This is the formula for positive predictive value.","This is the formula for sensitivity.","This is the formula for specificity.","Accuracy has correct results over everyone tested."],"k":"Know the formulas for all five measures.","rn":[["","What is the formula for specificity?","What is the formula for specificity?","TN / (TN + FP)"]]},{"id":46081,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A test's false negative count falls to zero, while the other cells are non-zero. What must be true?","o":["Sensitivity and negative predictive value are both 100%","Specificity and positive predictive value are both 100%","Accuracy is 100%, as no false results can remain anywhere","Prevalence must be 100%, since everyone has the disease","Positive predictive value is 100%, while sensitivity falls"],"e":"Sensitivity is TP / (TP + FN), and negative predictive value is TN / (FN + TN). With zero false negatives, both become 100%. False positives can remain, so specificity, positive predictive value and accuracy can all stay below 100%.","w":["These depend on false positives, which are non-zero.","False positives remain, so accuracy stays below 100%.","People without disease are still present in the table.","False positives remain, and sensitivity is 100% rather than lower."],"k":"FN = 0 → sensitivity and NPV are 100%.","rn":[["","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"],["","What is the formula for NPV (negative predictive value)?","What is the formula for NPV (negative predictive value)?","TN / (TN + FN)"],["","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","More true negatives per false negative; False negatives unlikely as disease is rare"]]},{"id":46082,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A test's false positive count falls to zero, while the other cells are non-zero. Which two measures become 100%?","o":["Specificity and positive predictive value","Sensitivity and negative predictive value","Sensitivity and specificity","Accuracy and negative predictive value","Prevalence and accuracy"],"e":"Specificity is TN / (FP + TN), and positive predictive value is TP / (TP + FP). With zero false positives, both are 100%. False negatives can remain, so sensitivity, negative predictive value and accuracy can stay below 100%.","w":["These measures depend on false negatives, which are non-zero.","Sensitivity depends on false negatives, which are non-zero.","Both of these depend on false negatives, which are non-zero.","Prevalence is not determined by the false positive count."],"k":"FP = 0 → specificity and PPV are 100%.","rn":[["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"],["","What is the formula for PPV (positive predictive value)?","What is the formula for PPV (positive predictive value)?","TP / (TP + FP)"],["","Heart failure test: TP 20, FP 2. What is the PPV (positive predictive value)?","Heart failure test: TP 20, FP 2. What is the PPV (positive predictive value)?","91%"]]},{"id":46083,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Test A has 99% sensitivity and 60% specificity. Test B has 60% sensitivity and 99% specificity. Which option applies the principles of screening and diagnosis?","o":["A suits screening, and B suits confirming a diagnosis","B suits screening, and A suits confirming a diagnosis","A suits both, because sensitivity is all that matters","B suits both, because specificity is all that matters","Neither suits anything, because they are below 100%"],"e":"Screening thresholds are generally chosen towards high sensitivity, in order not to miss potential disease. Diagnostic tests are chosen towards high specificity, with more weight on accuracy and precision. No test is 100% accurate, so this balance is unavoidable.","w":["This reverses the principles.","Diagnostic tests lean towards high specificity.","Screening leans towards high sensitivity.","No test is 100% accurate, so falling below 100% does not rule a test out."],"k":"High-sensitivity test to screen; high-specificity test to confirm.","rn":[["","Screening vs diagnostic: meaning of a positive result","Screening vs diagnostic: meaning of a positive result","Screening: suspicion of disease needing confirmation; Diagnostic: definite diagnosis"],["","Can a positive screening test confirm disease?","Can a positive screening test confirm disease?","No; Need a diagnostic test"]]},{"id":46084,"t":"Test Performance","s":"Which statement about a highly sensitive test is correct?","o":["Many subjects without the disease may test positive","Most subjects with the disease will test negative","A positive result gives certainty of the disease","A negative result gives no reassurance about disease","Only people who have symptoms will test positive"],"e":"High sensitivity means most people with the condition are identified. A negative result gives a high degree of certainty that the subject does not have the disease. A positive result may or may not mean disease, because many subjects without it may test positive as false positives.","w":["High sensitivity means most subjects with disease test positive.","A positive result on this test may or may not reflect disease.","A negative result on a highly sensitive test is reassuring.","Symptoms are not what the test result depends upon."],"k":"High sensitivity can mean more false positives.","rn":[["","High sensitivity: what may many people without the disease get?","High sensitivity: what may many people without the disease get?","False positives"],["","Heart failure test: 100 patients, 25 with disease. 20 test positive, 5 negative. Withou…","Heart failure test: 100 patients, 25 with disease. 20 test positive, 5 negative. Without disease, 2 test positive. How many true negatives?","73"],["","High sensitivity: what does a positive test mean?","High sensitivity: what does a positive test mean?","May or may not have disease"]]},{"id":46085,"t":"Test Performance","s":"Which statement about a highly specific test is correct?","o":["A negative result may or may not mean that the disease is absent","A positive result may or may not mean that the disease is present","Most people with the disease are always identified by the test","Many people without the disease will test positive on this test","A negative result on the test gives certainty that disease is absent"],"e":"High specificity means most subjects with a positive test do have the disease, so a positive result gives a high degree of certainty. If the test is negative, the subject may or may not have the disease. Identifying most people with the condition is the property of high sensitivity.","w":["A positive result on a specific test gives a high degree of certainty.","Identifying most people with disease is a property of high sensitivity.","High specificity means that false positives are few.","A negative result on a specific test is not conclusive."],"k":"High specificity: positive is convincing, negative is not.","rn":[["","High specificity: what does a negative test mean?","High specificity: what does a negative test mean?","May or may not have disease"],["","High sensitivity: what does a negative test mean?","High sensitivity: what does a negative test mean?","High certainty of no disease"],["","High specificity: what does a positive test mean?","High specificity: what does a positive test mean?","High certainty of disease"]]},{"id":46086,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A programme has a low positive predictive value. What is the main practical consequence?","o":["A lot of resource is used on diagnostic follow-up of false-positive patients","A lot of resource is saved, as nobody with a positive result needs any follow-up","Most people with a negative result actually have the disease and are missed","Most of the people with a positive result have the condition and need treatment","The sensitivity of the test falls because the disease has become more common"],"e":"With a low positive predictive value, a lot of resource is used on diagnostic follow-up of false-positive patients. With a high value, a higher proportion of people who test positive have the condition. So positive predictive value matters to patients and to services alike.","w":["All screen positives still need to be referred.","A low positive predictive value concerns positive results, not negatives.","This describes a high positive predictive value.","Sensitivity is not changed by a low positive predictive value."],"k":"Low PPV: resources spent on false-positive follow-up.","rn":[["","What does a low PPV (positive predictive value) imply?","What does a low PPV (positive predictive value) imply?","Much resource spent following up false positives"]]},{"id":46087,"t":"Test Performance","s":"A woman has a clear mammogram, and a week later she finds a lump. What is the best next step?","o":["Assess with a history and examination, with referral on a cancer pathway if concerned","Reassure and discharge, because a negative screening result rules out all breast disease","Wait until the next routine invitation, as screening has already been performed this year","Repeat the mammogram every day until one of the results comes back positive for cancer","Start cancer treatment immediately, because a lump alone is a definite diagnosis"],"e":"Tests are not taken alone, so always take a history and do an examination. A patient with a clear mammogram who finds a lump a week later still needs assessing, and a concerned general practitioner can refer them on a cancer pathway. No test is 100% accurate, and symptoms move the person out of the screening setting.","w":["No test is 100% accurate, and false negatives can occur.","Symptoms need assessment now, and should not wait for the next invitation.","Repeated screening until positive is not the correct approach.","A diagnosis needs confirmation by a test such as a biopsy."],"k":"Symptoms after a negative screen still need assessment.","rn":[["","A patient has a clear mammogram but a lump: what else must the GP do?","A patient has a clear mammogram but a lump: what else must the GP do?","History and examination"]]},{"id":46088,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"A person has a false-negative screening result. Which step is the programme's main way of finding these errors?","a":["reporting of outcomes","outcome reporting","reporting outcomes","reporting of outcome","report outcomes"],"e":"Reporting of outcomes involves collecting, analysing and reporting on outcomes to identify false negatives. This improves the effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of the programme, and it also justifies having the programme. False positives are identified at the diagnosis step.","k":"False negatives are identified by reporting of outcomes.","rn":[["","Screening programme step 7: what does reporting of outcomes involve?","Screening programme step 7: what does reporting of outcomes involve?","Collect, analyse and report outcomes; To identify false negatives; To improve effectiveness and cost-effectiveness"],["","What 2 reasons are outcomes reported in screening programmes?","What 2 reasons are outcomes reported in screening programmes?","Identify false negatives; Improve effectiveness and cost-effectiveness"]]},{"id":46089,"t":"Types of Screening","s":"A primary care nurse measures a raised blood pressure at a health check in a patient who feels fine. What is the most appropriate next action, and why is this valuable?","o":["Repeat the reading and discuss diet and family history, as hypertension can be silent","Diagnose hypertension from the single reading and start lifelong treatment straight away","Ignore the reading, because a person without symptoms cannot have any significant disease","Refer directly for rehabilitation, as this reading is an example of tertiary prevention","Remove the patient from all later screening, since a condition has now been identified"],"e":"A health check can pick up people with silent hypertension. A high reading is repeated, and diet and family history are discussed. A screening result is not a diagnosis, so a single reading should not be treated as definite. Absence of symptoms does not exclude disease.","w":["A screening finding is not a definite diagnosis.","Silent hypertension is exactly what such a check can pick up.","Rehabilitation is a tertiary measure for established disease.","Finding a raised reading is not a reason to remove someone from screening."],"k":"Health checks can pick up silent hypertension; repeat high readings.","rn":[["","Why is the NHS health check worthwhile?","Why is the NHS health check worthwhile?","Picks up silent hypertension"]]},{"id":46090,"t":"Types of Screening","s":"A programme tests only people in a particular high-risk group, rather than everyone. Which type of screening is this?","a":["selective","selective screening","targeted","targeted screening"],"e":"Selective screening is applied to a particular subset of the population, such as a high-risk group. Population-based screening instead systematically covers every person within a population. Selective screening can also be multiphasic, since the types are not mutually exclusive.","k":"Selective = a subset such as a high-risk group.","rn":[["","What is selective screening?","What is selective screening?","Applied to a particular subset, e.g. high-risk group"]]},{"id":46091,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A person without a working spleen receives vaccinations to manage their risk. Why does this example show the overlap of categories?","o":["Vaccination is primary in healthy people, but here it is listed as secondary risk factor management","Vaccination is always primordial, whatever the patient's condition may be, because vaccines are public policy","Vaccination is always quaternary, since a person without a spleen has illness but no disease","Vaccination is never considered to be prevention, because it does not detect any disease early","Vaccination is always tertiary, because all asplenic patients are already in rehabilitation"],"e":"Vaccination generally appears under primary prevention, reducing exposure to risk factors in healthy people. Vaccinations in asplenic individuals are listed under risk factor management in secondary prevention. Classification systems are imperfect and interventions may fall into more than one category.","w":["Primordial prevention acts through social and environmental measures in a population.","Quaternary prevention concerns protection from over-medicalisation.","Vaccination is listed under both primary and secondary prevention.","Tertiary prevention is not the listed category for this example."],"k":"Same intervention, different level: vaccination is primary or secondary.","rn":[["","What are 3 risk factor management examples of secondary prevention?","What are 3 risk factor management examples of secondary prevention?","Smoking cessation; Vaccinations in asplenic individuals; Foot check for diabetics"],["","Sunscreens and vaccinations are examples of which primary prevention approach?","Sunscreens and vaccinations are examples of which primary prevention approach?","Reduced exposure to risk factors"],["","What are the 2 approaches of primary prevention?","What are the 2 approaches of primary prevention?","Reduce exposure to risk factors; Identify and manage risk factors"]]},{"id":46092,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"Which of these is a primary prevention measure, rather than primordial?","o":["Sunscreen for individuals at risk of skin damage","Legislation to ban indoor smoking","Better quality housing for a population","Building new bike trails across a city","State-wide tobacco control measures"],"e":"Primary prevention targets a susceptible population or individual, as with sunscreens, vaccinations and smoking cessation. Primordial prevention targets the population through social and environmental interventions, such as legislation, housing, trails and tobacco control. The other four options are therefore primordial.","w":["Legislation to ban indoor smoking is primordial.","Improving the quality of housing is primordial.","Building bike or pedestrian trails is primordial.","State tobacco control measures are primordial."],"k":"Sunscreen: primary. Laws, housing, trails: primordial.","rn":[["","Sunscreens and vaccinations are examples of which primary prevention approach?","Sunscreens and vaccinations are examples of which primary prevention approach?","Reduced exposure to risk factors"]]},{"id":46093,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"Which term describes the lasting after-effects of disease that tertiary prevention tries to reduce?","a":["sequelae","sequela","complications"],"e":"Sequelae are lasting after-effects of a disease. Tertiary prevention aims to reduce disease severity and associated complications or sequelae. It targets the clinical and outcome stages, for example through rehabilitation after a stroke or chronic disease management.","k":"Sequelae = lasting after-effects of disease.","rn":[["","What does tertiary prevention reduce?","What does tertiary prevention reduce?","Disease severity; Associated complications/sequelae"]]},{"id":46094,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A doctor matches each type of prevention to the natural history stage it targets. Which match is correct?","o":["Secondary: subclinical stage","Primary: clinical stage","Primordial: susceptible stage","Tertiary: underlying stage","Secondary: outcome stage"],"e":"The types of prevention mirror the natural history. Primordial targets underlying, primary targets susceptible, secondary targets subclinical, and tertiary targets clinical and outcome. Quaternary applies to illness with no disease.","w":["Primary targets the susceptible stage.","Primordial targets the underlying stage.","Tertiary targets the clinical and outcome stages.","Secondary targets the subclinical stage."],"k":"Prevention mirrors natural history.","rn":[["","Secondary prevention targets who?","Secondary prevention targets who?","Individuals with subclinical disease"]]},{"id":46095,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A patient with symptoms of a stroke two years ago attends a long-term programme to reduce their disability. At what natural history stage and prevention level are they?","o":["Clinical or outcome stage; tertiary prevention","Susceptible stage; primary prevention","Underlying stage; primordial prevention","Subclinical stage; secondary prevention","Subclinical stage; quaternary prevention"],"e":"The person has had symptoms and now has lasting effects, so they are in the clinical or outcome stage. Tertiary prevention targets these stages and aims to reduce severity and complications. Rehabilitation after stroke is one of the examples given.","w":["A susceptible person has risk factors without disease.","The underlying stage comes before risk factors exist.","A subclinical person has no symptoms.","Quaternary prevention concerns illness without disease."],"k":"Rehabilitation after stroke: clinical/outcome stage, tertiary.","rn":[["","Tertiary prevention targets which stages of disease?","Tertiary prevention targets which stages of disease?","Clinical; Outcome"]]},{"id":46096,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A city improves its water supplies so that disease does not emerge in its generally healthy residents. Which level of prevention best describes this?","o":["Primary prevention","Secondary prevention","Tertiary prevention","Quaternary prevention","It is not a form of prevention"],"e":"Other examples listed under primary prevention include decreasing environmental risks, enhancing nutritional status, immunising against communicable diseases and improving water supplies. These aim to prevent a disease emerging. The classification is imperfect, and such examples may overlap with population-level measures.","w":["Secondary prevention targets subclinical disease through early detection.","Tertiary prevention targets established disease.","Quaternary prevention protects patients from over-medicalisation.","Water supplies are listed as a form of prevention."],"k":"Improving water supplies is listed under primary prevention.","rn":[["","What are 4 examples of primary prevention at that level?","What are 4 examples of primary prevention at that level?","Decreasing environmental risks; Enhancing nutritional status; Immunising against communicable diseases; Improving water supplies"]]},{"id":46097,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"Why are registers needed when identifying the population eligible for screening?","o":["To ensure that people's details are collected and kept up to date","To store final diagnoses in place of a gold standard test","To remove everyone at low risk from the programme permanently","To record which patients have already developed symptoms","To calculate each person's sensitivity and specificity"],"e":"The first step is to determine the group to be screened based on best evidence. Registers are used to make sure people's details are collected and up to date. This allows the full cohort to be invited. Results follow you around the UK, so you are called in the same way wherever you live.","w":["Registers do not replace a gold standard test.","Registers are not used for permanently removing low-risk people.","Registers are for eligible people and not chiefly a record of symptoms.","Sensitivity and specificity are properties of tests, not people."],"k":"Registers keep eligible people's details current.","rn":[["","Screening programme step 1: how is the eligible population identified?","Screening programme step 1: how is the eligible population identified?","Group chosen based on best evidence; Registers keep people's details up to date"]]},{"id":46098,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"In a programme, positive results are referred promptly, but people with negative results hear nothing. Which requirement is not being met?","o":["Screen-negative results must be reported to individuals","Screen-negative results must go to gold standard testing","Screen-positive results must be withheld from the services","Screen-negative results must be recorded as false positives","Screen-positive results must be reported only after treatment"],"e":"At the referral and reporting stage, all screen-positive results are referred to appropriate services, and screen negatives are reported to individuals. The next steps are diagnosis and then intervention, treatment and follow-up. Everyone tested therefore gets a result.","w":["Gold standard testing is for positive or suspicious findings.","Screen positives must be referred to appropriate services.","A false positive is a positive test without disease, so negatives cannot be recorded as these.","Positive results go to services before treatment can happen."],"k":"Refer positives; report negatives to individuals.","rn":[["","Screening programme step 4: what happens to screen-positive and screen-negative results?","Screening programme step 4: what happens to screen-positive and screen-negative results?","All screen-positives referred to appropriate services; Screen-negatives reported to individuals"]]},{"id":46099,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"In some screened conditions, what additional step may be required after intervention or treatment?","a":["surveillance","follow-up","follow up","surveillance or follow-up","surveillance and follow-up"],"e":"At the intervention stage, cases are intervened upon or treated appropriately. In some conditions, surveillance or follow-up will also be required. Outcomes are then collected, analysed and reported to improve the programme.","k":"Some conditions need surveillance or follow-up after treatment.","rn":[["","Screening programme step 6: what happens in intervention, treatment and follow-up?","Screening programme step 6: what happens in intervention, treatment and follow-up?","Cases intervened on or treated appropriately; Some conditions also need surveillance or follow-up"]]},{"id":46100,"t":"Implementing Screening","s":"From which earlier set of principles did the criteria used by the National Screening Committee evolve?","a":["wilson-jungner","wilson and jungner","wilson jungner","wilson-jungner criteria","wilson and jungner criteria","wilson jungner criteria"],"e":"The National Screening Committee considers all the evidence and makes recommendations based on a set of criteria that evolved from the Wilson-Jungner criteria. The originals are ten criteria covering the condition, test, treatment and programme. They were published by the World Health Organisation.","k":"National criteria evolved from Wilson-Jungner.","rn":[["","What does the National Screening Committee base its recommendations on?","What does the National Screening Committee base its recommendations on?","All the evidence; Criteria that evolved from the Wilson-Jungner criteria"],["","What are the criteria for screening called?","What are the criteria for screening called?","Wilson-Jungner criteria"]]},{"id":46101,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"A proposed screening programme is accurate, but it needs highly specialised staff and can reach only a small number of people. Which general requirement of screening does this break?","o":["It must be accessible, affordable and easy to apply to a large section of the population","It must be invasive, because accuracy always comes first in a population programme","It must only be applied to symptomatic people who are unable to access other services","It must be limited to rare conditions, as screening is less important for common ones","It must always give a definite diagnosis so that specialised staff are not needed later"],"e":"A screening programme has to be accessible, affordable and easy to apply to a large section of the population. Screening tests should be simple, cheap and acceptable to patients and staff. Greater weight on accuracy and precision over acceptability is a feature of diagnostic tests instead.","w":["Screening tests should be simple and not harmful.","Screening is applied to asymptomatic people.","The condition should be an important health problem.","A screening test is not intended to be diagnostic."],"k":"Screening must be accessible, affordable and easy to apply widely.","rn":[["","What 3 things must a screening programme be?","What 3 things must a screening programme be?","Accessible; Affordable; Easy to apply to a large population"]]},{"id":46102,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Screening is often pictured as a sieve, with well-looking people going in and some being caught. What does this image correctly convey?","o":["The test sorts people, but it does not tell you who truly has the disease","The test tells you exactly who has the disease and leaves no doubt about it","The test removes the disease from anyone who passes through the sieve","The test applies only to those who already have symptoms of the disease","The test lets through only those people who definitely have the disease"],"e":"Screening is a rough sorting process, like a sieve. It sorts people into screen negative and screen positive, but it only provides a probability of potentially having a disease. It does not tell you who truly has the disease. Positives must go on to the gold standard.","w":["Screening is not 100% accurate, and it gives only a probability.","A test does not remove any disease.","Screening is applied to apparently well people.","Screen positives may be false positives."],"k":"Screening sorts; it does not diagnose.","rn":[["","Screening tests sort apparently well persons into which 2 groups?","Screening tests sort apparently well persons into which 2 groups?","Probably have a disease; Probably do not"]]},{"id":46103,"t":"Test Performance","s":"A student works out sensitivity using everyone in a study, including those without disease. Why is this wrong?","o":["Sensitivity uses only those who have the disease, reading the \"has disease\" column","Sensitivity uses only those who test positive, reading along the positive result row","Sensitivity uses only those who test negative, reading along the negative result row","Sensitivity uses only those who have no disease, reading the \"no disease\" column","Sensitivity requires the disease prevalence, and it cannot be calculated from any table"],"e":"Sensitivity is TP / (TP + FN), and it reads down the \"has disease\" column. It is the ability of a test to identify correctly all people with the condition. Specificity reads down the \"no disease\" column, while predictive values read across rows. Using the whole table gives accuracy.","w":["Reading along the positive row gives positive predictive value.","Reading along the negative row gives negative predictive value.","Reading the \"no disease\" column gives specificity.","Sensitivity comes directly from the table, with no need to know population prevalence."],"k":"Sensitivity: only the people with disease.","rn":[["","High sensitivity: what may many people without the disease get?","High sensitivity: what may many people without the disease get?","False positives"],["","High sensitivity: what does a positive test mean?","High sensitivity: what does a positive test mean?","May or may not have disease"],["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"]]},{"id":46104,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"Two groups have the same test, but one has far more disease. What would you expect for the test's sensitivity and specificity if the test performs the same in each?","o":["They stay the same, while the predictive values change","They both rise, while the predictive values stay the same","They both fall, while the predictive values stay the same","Only specificity rises, because there are more true negatives","Only sensitivity falls, because there are more false negatives"],"e":"Sensitivity reads within those with disease and specificity reads within those without it, so changing how common the disease is does not change these proportions. Predictive values, however, are influenced by disease prevalence. This is why the same test can have a very different positive predictive value in different places.","w":["Predictive values are the measures that shift with prevalence.","Predictive values are the measures that shift with prevalence.","Specificity is measured only among those without disease.","Sensitivity is measured only among those with disease."],"k":"Sensitivity and specificity hold; predictive values shift with prevalence."},{"id":46105,"t":"Test Performance","s":"A question asks for the number of people with a condition. Which term is correct, and what does the term \"incidence\" refer to instead?","o":["Prevalence; incidence means new cases of a disease","Incidence; prevalence means new cases of a disease","Prevalence; incidence means deaths from a condition","Mortality; incidence means people with risk factors","Sensitivity; incidence means true positive results"],"e":"Prevalence is the number of people with the condition. Incidence refers to new cases of a disease. Mortality refers to deaths from a condition. Screening aims can target each of these, and prevalence is what affects predictive values.","w":["This reverses the two terms.","Mortality means deaths from a condition.","Mortality means deaths, and incidence does not mean risk factors.","Sensitivity is a property of a test, and incidence is not true positives."],"k":"Prevalence: people with it. Incidence: new cases. Mortality: deaths.","rn":[["","High sensitivity: what does a positive test mean?","High sensitivity: what does a positive test mean?","May or may not have disease"],["","High specificity: what does a negative test mean?","High specificity: what does a negative test mean?","May or may not have disease"],["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"]]},{"id":46106,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A test is carried out on a person with a risk factor, before any detectable tissue changes exist. At which level of prevention does this work?","a":["primary","primary prevention"],"e":"When screening is done before there are detectable tissue changes, it is working at the level of primary prevention. Most screening sits in secondary prevention, where there is subclinical disease. This shows that the categories overlap.","k":"Screening before tissue changes = primary prevention.","rn":[["","Is all screening secondary prevention?","Is all screening secondary prevention?","No; Can be primary if before tissue changes"]]},{"id":46107,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"Which option is a screening programme listed under secondary prevention?","o":["Neonatal hearing and dental checks","A ban on indoor smoking in public places","Rehabilitation following a stroke","Sunscreen for healthy people","Protection from over-medicalisation"],"e":"Secondary prevention includes screening programmes such as cervical cancer, aortic aneurysm, and neonatal hearing and dental checks. Its emphasis is on early detection in subclinical disease. Risk factor management, such as foot checks in diabetics, also sits at this level.","w":["This is primordial prevention.","This is tertiary prevention.","This is primary prevention.","This is quaternary prevention."],"k":"Secondary examples: cervical, aortic aneurysm, neonatal checks.","rn":[["","What are 4 screening programme examples of secondary prevention?","What are 4 screening programme examples of secondary prevention?","Cervical cancer; Aortic aneurysm; Neonatal hearing; Dental checks"]]},{"id":46108,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A couple planning a pregnancy stop smoking, cut alcohol and take vitamins before conception. Why does this fit the idea of acting very early?","o":["Cardiovascular disease starts in early life, with fetal and infant health","Cardiovascular disease only starts after symptoms occur in adulthood","Risk factors matter only once a person has a diagnosed condition","Fetal exposures are unrelated to lifetime risk and are not relevant","Individual exposures do not change a person's accumulated lifetime risk of disease"],"e":"Cardiovascular disease starts in early life, with fetal and infant health. Maternal adverse exposures include smoking, unhealthy diet and non-ideal body weight. Stopping smoking, cutting alcohol and taking vitamins before a planned pregnancy was raised as a way of acting early, before risk factors develop.","w":["Risk builds up long before symptoms appear.","Prevention aims to act before a condition is diagnosed.","Fetal exposures contribute to accumulated risk.","Individual risks vary according to life experiences and exposure history."],"k":"Lifetime cardiovascular risk begins in fetal life.","rn":[["","Cardiovascular disease starts when?","Cardiovascular disease starts when?","Early life; Fetal and infant health"]]},{"id":46109,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"Which item is a maternal risk factor during pregnancy for accumulated cardiovascular risk, rather than a general adulthood exposure?","o":["Pre-eclampsia","Psychosocial stress","Occupational exposures","Environmental chemicals","Physical inactivity"],"e":"Maternal risk factors during pregnancy include gestational diabetes mellitus, dyslipidaemia, familial hypercholesterolaemia, hypertension, genetics and high-risk populations, and pre-eclampsia. The general adulthood exposures include psychosocial stress, occupational exposures and chemical and environmental exposure.","w":["Psychosocial stress is an adulthood exposure.","Occupational exposures are an adulthood exposure.","Chemical and environmental exposure is an adulthood exposure.","Physical inactivity is a general lifestyle exposure."],"k":"Pre-eclampsia is a maternal pregnancy risk factor.","rn":[["","Which maternal risk factors during pregnancy matter for CVD (cardiovascular disease) pr…","Which maternal risk factors during pregnancy matter for CVD (cardiovascular disease) prevention? (part 2)","Hypertension; Genetics and high-risk populations; Pre-eclampsia"]]},{"id":46110,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Why is a positive screening result often used in combination with other risk factors?","o":["It only indicates suspicion of disease that warrants confirmation","It proves the disease on its own, so risk factors are irrelevant","It rules out disease completely if no risk factor is present","It is used to calculate the patient's own personal sensitivity","It is a treatment decision that needs no further assessment"],"e":"A positive screening result essentially indicates suspicion of disease, often used in combination with other risk factors, that warrants confirmation. In contrast, a diagnostic test result provides a definite diagnosis. So the screening result is one piece of a wider assessment.","w":["A positive screen does not prove disease.","A positive result cannot rule out disease.","Sensitivity describes a test, not an individual patient.","Further assessment is needed for a positive result."],"k":"Positive screen = suspicion, combined with other risk factors.","rn":[["","Screening vs diagnostic: meaning of a positive result","Screening vs diagnostic: meaning of a positive result","Screening: suspicion of disease needing confirmation; Diagnostic: definite diagnosis"]]},{"id":46111,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Which group is the appropriate target of a screening test?","o":["Large numbers of asymptomatic but potentially at-risk people","Small numbers of people with obvious symptoms of the disease","Only those with a positive result on another screening test","Only those patients who have had a confirmed biopsy result","Only people with established disease attending rehabilitation"],"e":"Screening tests target large numbers of asymptomatic but potentially at-risk individuals. Diagnostic tests target symptomatic individuals and asymptomatic individuals with a positive screening test. Patients in rehabilitation are in the setting of tertiary prevention.","w":["Symptomatic people should have diagnostic tests.","People with a positive screen go on to diagnostic testing.","A confirmed biopsy result means a diagnosis has already been made.","Rehabilitation is a tertiary prevention setting."],"k":"Screening target: many asymptomatic, potentially at-risk people.","rn":[["","Screening vs diagnostic: target population","Screening vs diagnostic: target population","Screening: large numbers, asymptomatic, potentially at risk; Diagnostic: symptomatic, or positive screening test"]]},{"id":46112,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Which statement about tests in practice is correct?","o":["No test is 100% accurate, so results must be interpreted with care","A screening test is always 100% accurate when applied to a well person","A test with high sensitivity will always have high specificity as well","A test with high specificity will always have high predictive values","A gold standard test makes taking a history or examining someone unnecessary"],"e":"No test is 100% accurate. Tests are not taken alone, and a history and examination should always be taken. High sensitivity and high specificity are separate properties, and predictive values also depend on disease prevalence.","w":["Screening is not 100% accurate.","Sensitivity and specificity are separate properties.","Predictive values also depend on prevalence.","Tests are not taken alone."],"k":"No test is 100% accurate; tests are not taken alone.","rn":[["","Is screening 100% accurate?","Is screening 100% accurate?","No; Gives a probability of disease"]]},{"id":46113,"t":"Test Performance","s":"A test with 95% sensitivity is used on 400 people who have the disease. How many true positives are expected?","a":["380","380 people","380 true positives"],"e":"Sensitivity is TP / (TP + FN), which is the share of people with the disease who test positive. Here 0.95 x 400 = 380 true positives, leaving 20 false negatives. People without the disease do not enter this calculation.","k":"True positives = sensitivity x number with disease."},{"id":46114,"t":"Implementing Screening","s":"A woman is invited for screening and asks about false-positive results. Why is it right to discuss these honestly?","o":["Informed choice and managing expectations are key issues in screening","Screening is compulsory, so her own opinion does not affect anything","False-positive results occur only with diagnostic tests and not in screening","Discussing false positives will raise the sensitivity of her screening test","Honest information is relevant only to those people who have symptoms"],"e":"Key issues regarding screening include how to ensure people are able to make informed choices and how to manage public expectations. The invitation stage supplies information so that people can make an informed choice to participate. Screening has costs and downsides as well as benefits.","w":["Information is given to enable an informed choice to participate.","Screening tests produce false positives.","A discussion does not change the sensitivity of a test.","Those invited for screening are generally without symptoms."],"k":"Honest information supports informed choice and realistic expectations.","rn":[["","What are the 3 key issues regarding screening?","What are the 3 key issues regarding screening?","Small mortality benefits vs public money; Ensuring informed choices; Managing public expectations"]]},{"id":46115,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Clinicians cannot agree which screen-detected patients should actually receive treatment. Which criterion is unmet?","o":["An agreed policy on whom to treat as patients","A suitable test or examination for the condition","A condition that is an important health problem","A test that is acceptable to the population","A cost that is economically balanced"],"e":"Criterion 8 states that there should be an agreed policy on whom to treat as patients. Without it, detection does not lead to clear action. The other options are separate criteria and are not the issue described.","w":["The suitability of the test is not the issue described.","The importance of the condition is not the issue described.","The acceptability of the test is not the issue described.","The cost is not the issue described."],"k":"There must be an agreed policy on whom to treat."},{"id":46116,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"The cost of a programme must be judged including which of the following?","o":["Diagnosis and treatment of patients diagnosed, balanced against medical care as a whole","Only the price of the first test, without considering what happens to those testing positive","Only the cost of letters sent, as the cost of treatment falls outside the programme","Only the cost to the people who test negative, since they are the largest group tested","Only the cost of treating people who have symptoms before the programme has started"],"e":"Criterion 9 states that the cost of case-finding, including diagnosis and treatment of patients diagnosed, should be economically balanced in relation to possible expenditure on medical care as a whole. Screening tests are cheap individually, but the whole pathway must be justified.","w":["The cost must include the diagnosis and treatment of those found.","The cost must include the diagnosis and treatment of those found.","The criterion concerns the whole cost of case-finding.","The criterion concerns case-finding and those diagnosed through it."],"k":"Cost includes diagnosis and treatment, balanced against all medical care.","rn":[["","What must happen to persons with positive or suspicious findings?","What must happen to persons with positive or suspicious findings?","Referred to their physician; For diagnosis and necessary treatment"]]},{"id":46117,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"A diagnostic test is uncomfortable and costly. Why might it still be appropriate?","o":["It is justifiable as necessary to establish the diagnosis","It is required to replace all screening of well populations","It should be cheap and simple, and discomfort proves its worth","It is designed for use on large numbers of healthy people","It gives more weight to patient acceptability than to accuracy"],"e":"Diagnostic tests may be invasive and expensive, but this is justifiable as necessary to establish a diagnosis. More weight is given to accuracy and precision than to patient acceptability. They are used for symptomatic individuals or asymptomatic individuals with a positive screening test.","w":["Diagnostic tests do not replace screening.","Simple and cheap describes screening tests.","Large numbers of asymptomatic people are the target of screening tests.","More weight is given to accuracy and precision than to acceptability."],"k":"Invasive, costly diagnostic tests are justified to establish a diagnosis.","rn":[["","What must happen to persons with positive or suspicious findings?","What must happen to persons with positive or suspicious findings?","Referred to their physician; For diagnosis and necessary treatment"]]},{"id":46118,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"Which option is an example of smoking-related secondary prevention?","o":["Offering lung screening to people at high risk of lung cancer","Setting age limits on the sale of cigarettes in shops","Treating smoking-related cardiovascular disease that has developed","Banning smoking in cars with children as a piece of legislation","Protecting an unwell non-smoker from over-medicalisation"],"e":"In the smoking example, secondary prevention means people at high risk of lung cancer are offered screening. Age limits on sale are primary prevention in this classification. Reducing the impact once disease has developed is tertiary. Legislation is a primordial measure.","w":["Age limits aim to prevent starting, which is primary.","Reducing the impact of developed disease is tertiary.","Legislation on smoking in cars is primordial.","Protection from over-medicalisation is quaternary."],"k":"Smoking example: lung screening for the high-risk is secondary.","rn":[["","Smoking as secondary prevention?","Smoking as secondary prevention?","Lung cancer screening for high-risk people"]]},{"id":46119,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"Which term describes a disease that has started, but gives no symptoms yet?","a":["subclinical","sub-clinical","subclinical disease"],"e":"Subclinical means that disease has started but gives no symptoms yet. Asymptomatic means having no symptoms, so the person feels fine. The subclinical stage begins with the onset of pathological changes, and it is the target of secondary prevention.","k":"Subclinical = disease present, no symptoms.","rn":[["","Secondary prevention targets who?","Secondary prevention targets who?","Individuals with subclinical disease"]]},{"id":46120,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"Which statement about who benefits from screening is most accurate?","o":["Better outcomes may follow for some of the screened individuals","Better outcomes are guaranteed for every screened individual","Better outcomes follow only for those who test negative","Better outcomes follow only for those who have false-positive results","Better outcomes never follow, because early treatment cannot help"],"e":"Screening identifies higher-risk people so that early treatment or intervention can be offered. This may lead to better health outcomes for some of the screened individuals, not all. The benefits do not always outweigh the downsides, and some programmes have small benefits in terms of mortality.","w":["Benefit is possible for some, and is not guaranteed.","Early intervention is offered to people found to be at higher risk.","False positives lead to follow-up and do not bring the intended benefit.","Early treatment is the means by which screening can improve outcomes."],"k":"Screening may help some, not all.","rn":[["","What may screening lead to for some screened individuals?","What may screening lead to for some screened individuals?","Better health outcomes"]]},{"id":46121,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A 55-year-old feels well and has a positive result on a new screening test. The test has 90% sensitivity and 90% specificity. In the population screened, 1 person in every 100 has the disease. The patient is frightened and assumes that the chance of disease is 90%.<br><br>What is the approximate chance that this patient has the disease, and why does it differ from their assumption?","o":["About 8%, because most positives are false when a disease is rare","About 90%, because sensitivity is the probability of disease after a positive test","About 50%, because a positive test can only be either true or false in each case","About 99%, because a test with good specificity makes a positive result nearly certain","About 1%, because a positive test does not change the patient's prevalence-based risk"],"e":"Step 1: per 10,000 people, 100 have disease, giving 90 true positives, and 9,900 do not, giving 990 false positives. Positive predictive value is 90 / 1,080, about 8%. Step 2: sensitivity describes people known to have the disease, not a person with a positive result. A positive result in a low-prevalence setting is potentially a false positive, so the patient should go on to the gold standard test.","w":["Sensitivity does not tell a patient what their own positive result means.","The probability is calculated from the table and is not simply one half.","Specificity alone does not make a positive result near certain when disease is rare.","A positive result does raise the probability above the prevalence."],"k":"PPV, not sensitivity, gives the meaning of a positive result.","rn":[["","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","More true negatives per false negative; False negatives unlikely as disease is rare"],["","High sensitivity: what may many people without the disease get?","High sensitivity: what may many people without the disease get?","False positives"]]},{"id":46122,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"The same antigen test is used in two towns. In Town A, the infection is common; in Town B, it is rare. The test's performance is unchanged between towns. A resident of each town has a positive result, and another resident of each has a negative result.<br><br>In which case is each result most trustworthy, and what explains this?","o":["Positive in Town A and negative in Town B, because prevalence shifts the predictive values","Positive in Town B and negative in Town A, because prevalence shifts the predictive values","Positive and negative in Town A, because higher prevalence improves both predictive values","Positive and negative in Town B, because lower prevalence improves both predictive values","Equal in both towns, because the test's sensitivity and specificity have stayed the same"],"e":"Step 1: with an unchanged test, sensitivity and specificity remain the same, but predictive values are influenced by prevalence. Step 2: as prevalence rises, positive predictive value rises and negative predictive value falls. So a positive result is more trustworthy where infection is common, and a negative result is more trustworthy where it is rare.","w":["This reverses the effect of prevalence on each predictive value.","Higher prevalence lowers the negative predictive value.","Lower prevalence lowers the positive predictive value.","Predictive values also depend on prevalence, not just on the test."],"k":"Common disease: trust a positive. Rare disease: trust a negative.","rn":[["","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","Why does NPV (negative predictive value) rise at low prevalence?","More true negatives per false negative; False negatives unlikely as disease is rare"],["","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","What 2 things influence NPV (negative predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"],["","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","What 2 things influence PPV (positive predictive value)?","Sensitivity/specificity of the test; Disease prevalence"]]},{"id":46123,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"A committee reviews a proposed screening programme for a serious, common condition. The natural history is well understood and has a long silent phase. A cheap and acceptable test exists. However, there is no accepted treatment for the disease, and no agreed policy on whom to treat.<br><br>What is the best conclusion, and which consequence is most likely if the programme went ahead?","o":["Criteria are unmet; people would be labelled with no effective action available","Criteria are met; a good test always justifies a programme, whatever follows it","Criteria are unmet; the only real problem is that the condition is far too common","Criteria are met; treatment is decided after a programme has been running for years","Criteria are unmet; the only real problem is that the silent phase is too long to find"],"e":"Step 1: the Wilson-Jungner criteria require an accepted treatment for recognised disease and an agreed policy on whom to treat, and neither is present. Step 2: the purpose of screening is to offer early treatment or intervention, so better outcomes could not follow. People would be identified with no effective action available, and benefits would not outweigh the downsides.","w":["A good test is only one of ten criteria.","The condition being an important health problem is a criterion in its favour.","Treatment and a policy on whom to treat should exist before starting.","A recognisable latent phase is a criterion that favours screening."],"k":"Without treatment or policy, screening cannot deliver its purpose.","rn":[["","How does screening reduce the severity of a condition?","How does screening reduce the severity of a condition?","Identifying people with the condition; Offering effective treatment"]]},{"id":46124,"t":"Test Performance","s":"A marker test is being set up for a large screening programme of asymptomatic adults. Lowering the positive cut-off catches nearly all cases but also flags many people without disease. A higher cut-off misses more cases but produces few false alarms. Positive results will all go on to a biopsy.<br><br>Which cut-off fits a screening test, and what is the trade-off?","o":["The lower cut-off, accepting more false positives so that disease is not missed","The higher cut-off, accepting more false negatives so that fewer people need any test","The lower cut-off, because it gives high specificity and makes the biopsy unnecessary","The higher cut-off, because a screening test must provide a definite diagnosis in everyone","Either cut-off, as a cut-off does not alter how many people are missed or wrongly flagged"],"e":"Step 1: screening thresholds are generally chosen towards high sensitivity, so as not to miss potential disease, which fits the lower cut-off. Step 2: the trade-off is more false positives, but a positive result only warrants confirmation, and the biopsy, as the gold standard, sorts true cases from false positives. Follow-up does use resources, which is why benefits must justify costs.","w":["Screening aims not to miss potential disease.","The lower cut-off leans towards sensitivity, and the biopsy is still needed.","A screening test is not intended to be diagnostic.","The scenario shows that the cut-off changes both error types."],"k":"Screening cut-offs favour sensitivity; confirmation handles false positives.","rn":[["","High sensitivity: what may many people without the disease get?","High sensitivity: what may many people without the disease get?","False positives"],["","Why is a screening threshold chosen towards high sensitivity?","Why is a screening threshold chosen towards high sensitivity?","Not to miss potential disease"]]},{"id":46125,"t":"What Is Screening","s":"A 52-year-old had a clear breast screening result last week. She now reports a new lump found at home. She is worried, but says that she has \"just been checked\". Her general practitioner takes a history and examines her, and the lump is confirmed.<br><br>Which option best explains the correct approach and the reasoning behind it?","o":["Refer her on a cancer pathway, because she has symptoms and no test is 100% accurate","Reassure her fully, because a clear result excludes any lump found in the following year","Wait for her next invitation, because screening services are responsible for any follow-up","Repeat the same screening test only, because the first result must have been a false positive","Diagnose cancer at once, because a lump after a screening test is by definition a true positive"],"e":"Step 1: she now has a symptom, so she is no longer in the asymptomatic screening setting, and early diagnosis is a service for those with symptoms. Step 2: no test is 100% accurate and false negatives occur, so the earlier result cannot override the finding. Tests are not taken alone, and a concerned general practitioner can refer on a cancer pathway.","w":["False negatives occur, so a clear result does not exclude disease.","A new symptom needs assessment now, and should not wait for a routine invitation.","A clear result that may be wrong would be a false negative, not a false positive.","A diagnosis needs confirmation with a diagnostic test."],"k":"Symptoms override a recent negative screen.","rn":[["","Is screening 100% accurate?","Is screening 100% accurate?","No; Gives a probability of disease"],["","PSA (prostate-specific antigen) test example: how many have cancer cells on biopsy, and…","PSA (prostate-specific antigen) test example: how many have cancer cells on biopsy, and how many do not?","Present: 100; Absent: 900"]]},{"id":46126,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A country considers four actions on smoking and lung disease: raising the legal age of cigarette sale; offering lung screening to very heavy smokers; rehabilitation after lung cancer treatment; and a review to stop unhelpful extra investigations in a patient who has symptoms but no disease.<br><br>Which option correctly labels them in order, and what does this show?","o":["Primary, secondary, tertiary, quaternary; a topic can span several levels","Primordial, tertiary, secondary, primary; each topic fits one level only","Secondary, primary, quaternary, tertiary; a topic can span several levels","Primary, tertiary, secondary, quaternary; each topic fits one level only","Tertiary, secondary, primary, primordial; levels never overlap in practice"],"e":"Step 1: age limits stop people starting, which is primary in the smoking example. Screening those at high risk is secondary, rehabilitation is tertiary, and protecting a patient from over-medicalisation is quaternary. Step 2: classification systems are imperfect, and one health issue can be tackled at several levels, so the categories overlap.","w":["Screening is not tertiary, and topics can span several levels.","Age limits are not secondary, and screening is not primary.","Screening and rehabilitation are swapped here.","Rehabilitation is tertiary, and the levels can overlap."],"k":"One health issue can span all levels of prevention.","rn":[["","What are the 5 types of prevention, in order?","What are the 5 types of prevention, in order?","Primordial; Primary; Secondary; Tertiary; Quaternary"]]},{"id":46127,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"A screening programme invites only those people whose details happen to be available. Results are sent out, and positives are referred. Some people with negative results later develop the disease, but no data are ever collected on this. The programme says it is working because many people attend.<br><br>Which two parts of the flow are weakest, and what is the consequence?","o":["Population identification and outcome reporting; missed people and undetected false negatives","Testing and referral; every positive result would automatically become a false negative","Invitation and treatment; every negative result would automatically become a false positive","Diagnosis and intervention; the gold standard would no longer be necessary for any person","Testing and diagnosis; all patients would be better off, as false results would disappear"],"e":"Step 1: identifying the eligible population uses registers so that people's details are collected and up to date, and the full cohort should be invited. This is failing here. Step 2: reporting of outcomes identifies false negatives and improves effectiveness and cost-effectiveness, and it also justifies the programme. Without this, attendance alone cannot show that the programme works.","w":["Positive results do not become false negatives, and these steps are described as working.","Negative results cannot become false positives.","Diagnosis and intervention are not the weakness, and the gold standard remains necessary.","False results do not disappear, and the failing steps are elsewhere."],"k":"Registers and outcome reporting underpin a working programme.","rn":[["","Screening programme step 7: what does reporting of outcomes involve?","Screening programme step 7: what does reporting of outcomes involve?","Collect, analyse and report outcomes; To identify false negatives; To improve effectiveness and cost-effectiveness"],["","What 2 reasons are outcomes reported in screening programmes?","What 2 reasons are outcomes reported in screening programmes?","Identify false negatives; Improve effectiveness and cost-effectiveness"]]},{"id":46128,"t":"Predictive Values and Prevalence","s":"A new test for a condition is studied in 1,000 people, with biopsy as the reference. Of 200 people with the disease, 190 test positive. Of 800 without the disease, 640 test negative. A patient asks what a positive result means for them, and another asks what a negative result means.<br><br>Which option gives the correct figures to use?","o":["Positive: about 54% chance of disease; negative: about 98.5% chance of being disease free","Positive: about 95% chance of disease; negative: about 80% chance of being disease free","Positive: about 80% chance of disease; negative: about 95% chance of being disease free","Positive: about 83% chance of disease; negative: about 83% chance of being disease free","Positive: about 20% chance of disease; negative: about 80% chance of being disease free"],"e":"Step 1: there are 190 true positives, 10 false negatives, 160 false positives and 640 true negatives. Step 2: patients need predictive values, not sensitivity and specificity. Positive predictive value is 190 / 350, about 54%. Negative predictive value is 640 / 650, about 98.5%. Sensitivity is 95%, specificity is 80%, and accuracy is 83%.","w":["These are the sensitivity and specificity.","These figures swap specificity and sensitivity into the wrong questions.","This is the accuracy, which does not answer either question.","These are the prevalence and specificity."],"k":"Patients' questions are answered by predictive values.","rn":[["","What 2 questions do sensitivity and specificity not answer?","What 2 questions do sensitivity and specificity not answer?","Positive result: probability of condition?; Negative result: probability of no disease?"],["","What is NPV (negative predictive value)?","What is NPV (negative predictive value)?","Probability of being disease free given a negative test"]]},{"id":46129,"t":"Aims of Screening","s":"A woman with long-standing diabetes who has no eye symptoms attends her yearly eye photograph appointment. Early changes are found at the back of her eye. She is referred, and treatment starts before her sight is affected.<br><br>Which aim of screening does this illustrate, and at what level of prevention does it act?","o":["Reducing severity by preventing blindness; secondary prevention","Increasing choice about a pregnancy; primordial prevention","Reducing incidence by treating a precursor; tertiary prevention","Reducing mortality from a cancer; quaternary prevention","Reducing prevalence by a change in law; primary prevention"],"e":"Step 1: diabetic retinopathy screening aims to reduce the severity of diabetic eye disease, through early detection and treatment to prevent blindness. Step 2: she has detectable changes but no symptoms, which is the subclinical stage. Screening programmes targeting that stage sit mainly in secondary prevention.","w":["Increasing choice is illustrated by antenatal screening.","Reducing incidence by treating precursors is illustrated by cervical screening.","Reducing mortality is illustrated by breast cancer screening.","A change in law is primordial prevention and is not the case here."],"k":"Diabetic eye screening: reduces severity; secondary prevention.","rn":[["","Image card: Which screening programme uses this eye image? What is its aim?","Which screening programme uses this eye image? What is its aim?","Diabetic retinopathy screening; Reduce severity, prevent blindness"],["","Diabetic retinopathy screening aims to prevent what?","Diabetic retinopathy screening aims to prevent what?","Blindness"]]},{"id":46130,"t":"Types of Prevention","s":"A 34-year-old has ongoing tiredness and low mood. Extensive tests have found no disease. Their doctor is asked to arrange a further invasive investigation \"just to be sure\", although it is very unlikely to help. The doctor instead explains this, avoids the test and arranges an ethically acceptable alternative.<br><br>Which level of prevention is this, and what harm is being avoided?","o":["Quaternary; harm from new medical invasion and unnecessary intervention","Primary; harm from a disease emerging in a susceptible healthy person","Secondary; harm from missing a subclinical disease at an early stage","Tertiary; harm from complications of a confirmed established disease","Primordial; harm from population risk factors in the wider environment"],"e":"Step 1: this is illness with no disease, which is the target of quaternary prevention. Step 2: the aim is to identify a patient at risk of over-medicalisation, protect them from new medical invasion and suggest interventions that are ethically acceptable. Further invasive testing here carries harm with little prospect of benefit.","w":["Primary prevention stops disease emerging, and this is not the concern here.","Secondary prevention looks for subclinical disease, whereas this doctor is limiting tests.","Tertiary prevention needs established disease, which has not been found.","Primordial prevention works at population level, not with an individual patient."],"k":"Quaternary prevention protects from over-medicalisation.","rn":[["","What 2 things does quaternary prevention do for a patient at risk?","What 2 things does quaternary prevention do for a patient at risk?","Protect from new medical invasion; Suggest ethically acceptable interventions"]]}]);
